{"raw": {"https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001232306506": {"title": "Director of Student Accounts (Bursar)", "description": "Director of Student Accounts (Bursar). Essential Duties & Responsibilities Provide strategic and operational leadership for the University\u2019s student accounts, billing, accounts receivable, cashiering, payment processing, refunds, collections, and reconciliation functions. Ensure accurate and timely assessment, processing, posting, reconciliation, and reporting of student financial transactions, including reconciliation of student accounts to the general ledger. Develop and execute the University\u2019s accounts receivable and collections strategy, including monitoring aging, delinquency, collection performance, and overall financial exposure. Oversee payment arrangements, account holds, write-offs, promissory notes, third-party billing, and external collection-agency relationships in accordance with University policy. Establish and maintain effective internal financial controls, including segregation of duties, approval authorities, reconciliations, documentation, cash controls, and appropriate audit trails. Ensure compliance with applicable federal and state requirements, Department of Education regulations, FERPA, PCI-DSS, University policies, and other requirements governing student financial operations. Partner with Finance and internal/external auditors on financial reviews, audit activities, year-end processes, corrective actions, and resolution of identified control or compliance issues. Identify, assess, and mitigate financial, operational, compliance, and control risks associated with Bursar operations. Lead, supervise, develop, and evaluate Bursar/Student Accounts staff; establish clear performance expectations, accountability, service standards, cross-training, and appropriate backup coverage. Serve as the functional leader for Bursar-related student information, accounts receivable, payment, and financial systems, partnering with Finance and IT on implementations, integrations, testing, upgrades, and optimization. Establish and monitor key performance indicators for receivables, aging, collections, billing and transaction accuracy, refunds, reconciliations, service performance, and other critical Bursar operations. Lead continuous improvement, automation, standardization, and process redesign initiatives to strengthen financial controls, operational efficiency, service delivery, and business continuity. Provide leadership and resolution for complex or escalated student financial matters, ensuring policies are applied consistently and students and families receive accurate, timely, and professional service. Partner with Financial Aid, Registrar, Enrollment Management, Finance, Academic Affairs, IT, and other University stakeholders to address systemic issues and ensure effective coordination across the student financial lifecycle. Provide University leadership with financial analysis, operational insights, risk assessments, and recommendations related to student receivables, financial operations, policy, systems, and opportunities for improvement. Education Bachelor\u2019s degree in Accounting, Finance, Business Administration, or a closely related field. Experience Minimum seven years of progressively responsible higher-education student financial services experience, including significant responsibility for student accounts, accounts receivable, billing, collections, refunds, and reconciliations. Minimum three years of demonstrated leadership experience in a Bursar, Deputy Bursar, Assistant Bursar, or comparable position with broad responsibility for student accounts, billing, receivables, collections, refunds, reconciliations, financial controls, compliance, and staff leadership. Minimum three years of direct supervisory/management experience, including responsibility for staff performance, development, and operational outcomes. Demonstrated experience managing the full student accounts lifecycle, rather than a single specialized component of Bursar operations. Demonstrated experience with financial controls, audit readiness, regulatory compliance, and operational risk management. Demonstrated experience analyzing accounts receivable aging and collection performance and implementing improvement strategies. Experience with student information systems, ERP/financial systems, electronic payment platforms, and related financial technology. Demonstrated experience leading significant process improvement, systems optimization, or operational change. Comparable titles may be considered when the candidate demonstrates equivalent breadth of responsibility, leadership accountability, decision authority, and Bursar-level operational experience."}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001232176700": {"title": "Sr. IT Support Lead", "description": "Sr. IT Support Lead. The Senior IT Support Lead serves as the senior technical resource for the IT Support team, delivering advanced technical support and operational leadership to faculty, staff, students, and authorized affiliates across Webster University. As a member of the Infrastructure & Operations team, this position provides advanced end-user support, service desk operations, classroom and audiovisual technology support, endpoint administration, and IT asset management while serving as the primary escalation point for complex technical issues and operational support activities. Essential Duties and Responsibilities* End User Support & Service Desk Respond to incidents, service requests, and technical support inquiries in accordance with established service level agreements (SLAs). Install, configure, maintain, troubleshoot, and support University-owned hardware, software, operating systems, mobile devices, printers, peripherals, and related technologies. Provide technical support through in-person, remote, telephone, email, and virtual support channels. This includes providing strong follow-up and claim ownership. Document, update, and maintain accurate records of incidents, service requests, assets, and work performed within the university's IT service management (ITSM) system. Diagnose and resolve hardware, software, network connectivity, account access, and other end-user technology issues, escalating requests when appropriate. Perform routine system maintenance, updates, equipment checks, and technology evaluations to ensure reliable service delivery. Classroom, AV, and Event Technology Support Serve as the primary technical escalation point for complex incidents and service requests. Provide technical support for classrooms, laboratories, conference rooms, and IT-supported events across Webster University locations. Install, configure, maintain, troubleshoot, and support audiovisual equipment including projectors, displays, microphones, speakers, cameras, control systems, and collaboration technologies. Support classroom instruction, webinars, hybrid meetings, livestreams, and other multimedia initiatives. Perform routine classroom technology inspections and coordinate equipment repairs, maintenance, and vendor service as needed. Assist with multimedia documentation, user training, and technology best practices. IT Asset Management & Technology Serve as the primary technical escalation point for complex incidents and service requests, providing advanced troubleshooting and quality assurance before escalating to other Information Technology teams. Maintain accurate inventory records for university-owned technology assets using approved asset management and IT service management systems. Support software implementation, asset tracking, procurement coordination, receiving, staging, tagging, deployment, surplus processing, and inventory reporting. Ensure asset records, inventory documentation, and related systems remain accurate, complete, and current. Independent Judgment and Decision-Making Exercise independent judgment and decision-making by carrying out process frameworks and adapting to real world scenarios. Lead or coordinate technology deployments, operating system upgrades, software rollouts, hardware refresh initiatives, and other campus technology projects. Serve as the primary technical escalation point for complex issues, and own escalations involving significant operational impacts. Mentor IT Support Technicians and student employees by providing technical guidance, knowledge sharing, and assistance with complex technical issues. Secondary Duties and Responsibilities* Serve as a secondary administrator for enterprise endpoint management platforms (e.g., Microsoft Intune, Ivanti, JAMF) by assisting with device administration, software deployments, policy testing, and endpoint management activities. Coordinate with vendors regarding equipment repairs, warranty claims, maintenance, break/fix services, product evaluations, and technical support requests. Test, evaluate, and validate new hardware, software, operating system updates, endpoint configurations, and other technologies before production deployment. Develop, review, and maintain technical documentation, knowledge base articles, standard operating procedures, and technical standards. Participate in lifecycle planning, deployment planning, technology evaluation, and other operational planning meetings while providing technical recommendations. Assist with hardware and software asset management activities, including inventory reconciliation, lifecycle tracking, procurement coordination, and technology deployment documentation. Participate in technology projects, continuous service improvement initiatives, and departmental professional development activities. Perform other duties as assigned. This position is generally scheduled to work Monday through Friday between 7:00 a.m. and 6:00 p.m. Regular, in-person attendance on Webster\u2019s campus is an essential function and physical requirement of the job. Work schedules are based on departmental and operational needs and may vary to support University operations. Occasional evening and weekend hours may be required to support classroom technology, IT-supported events, system implementations, maintenance activities, emergency response, and other operational requirements. This position is eligible for the University's Onsite Flex Work program in accordance with university policy and departmental operational requirements. Participation is based on job responsibilities, operational needs, and supervisory approval. *Any one position may not include all of the duties listed, nor do the examples necessarily include all of the duties performed. Functional Skills Can perform excellent written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills, including but not limited to; Teams, phone, email, in person, and the ticket management system. Demonstrates strong ticket management and communication skills by thoroughly documenting troubleshooting steps, actions taken, findings, and resolutions to ensure accurate case tracking, knowledge sharing, and seamless issue handoff when needed. Demonstrated commitment to providing exceptional customer service with professionalism, patience, and a customer-focused approach. Strong organizational, time management, and prioritization skills with the ability to manage multiple tasks and meet established deadlines. Ability to analyze problems, exercise sound judgment, and independently resolve technical issues while recognizing when escalation is appropriate. Ability to collaborate effectively within a team environment while also working independently with minimal supervision. Ability to adapt to changing technologies, priorities, and operational needs in a fast-paced environment. Ability to develop and maintain technical documentation, procedures, and knowledge resources. Ability to maintain confidentiality and safeguard sensitive information while complying with university policies and applicable regulations (e.g., FERPA, HIPAA, Title IX, and security awareness requirements). Requisite Experience* Bachelor's degree in Computer Science, Information Technology, Information Systems, or a related field preferred. Minimum of five (5) years of progressively responsible experience providing enterprise desktop support, service desk operations, endpoint management, classroom technology support, or related technical support services. Demonstrated experience serving as a technical lead, senior technician, or primary escalation point for complex technical issues. Experience working in a higher education or similarly complex customer-focused technology environment preferred. Experience providing advanced troubleshooting for Windows, macOS, Microsoft 365, endpoint management platforms, networking and cybersecurity basic fundamentals, audiovisual technologies, and enterprise business applications. Experience coordinating technology deployments, hardware refresh initiatives, software rollouts, operating system upgrades, or other campus technology projects. Experience working with endpoint management platforms such as Microsoft Intune, Ivanti, JAMF, or similar enterprise device management solutions. Experience coordinating with technology vendors regarding equipment repairs, warranty services, product evaluations, maintenance, and break/fix support. Experience developing and maintaining technical documentation, knowledge base articles, standard operating procedures, and technical standards. Experience supporting IT asset management activities, including hardware lifecycle management, inventory control, asset reconciliation, procurement coordination, and technology deployments. Experience testing, validating, and evaluating new hardware, software, operating system updates, endpoint policies, and enterprise technology solutions before production deployment."}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001232176300": {"title": "IT Support Technician", "description": "IT Support Technician. The IT Support Technician delivers comprehensive technical support and operational services to faculty, staff, students, and authorized affiliates across Webster University. As a member of the Infrastructure & Operations team, this role is responsible for providing end-user support, managing service desk operations, supporting classroom and audiovisual technologies, and assisting with IT asset management. The position plays a key role in ensuring the University\u2019s technology environment remains reliable, secure, and efficient. The IT Support Technician installs, configures, maintains, troubleshoots, and supports University-owned hardware, software, classroom technology, multimedia systems, and other IT-supported equipment while providing exceptional customer service through multiple service channels. This position also collaborates with Information Technology teams, campus departments, vendors, and student employees to support hardware and software deployments, asset lifecycle management, classroom instruction, IT-supported events, documentation, knowledge management, operational projects, and strategic technology initiatives across Webster University locations. Position-specific goals and objectives are established annually and align with the mission, vision, and strategic goals of Information Technology and Webster University. Essential Duties and Responsibilities* End User Support & Service Desk Respond to incidents, service requests, and technical support inquiries in accordance with established service level agreements (SLAs). Install, configure, maintain, troubleshoot, and support University-owned hardware, software, operating systems, mobile devices, printers, peripherals, and related technologies. Provide technical support through in-person, remote, telephone, email, and virtual support channels. This includes providing strong follow-up and claim ownership. Document, update, and maintain accurate records of incidents, service requests, assets, and work performed within the university's IT service management (ITSM) system. Diagnose and resolve hardware, software, network connectivity, account access, and other end-user technology issues, escalating requests when appropriate. Perform routine system maintenance, updates, equipment checks, and technology evaluations to ensure reliable service delivery. Classroom, AV, and Event Technology Support Provide technical support for classrooms, laboratories, conference rooms, and IT-supported events across Webster University locations. Install, configure, maintain, troubleshoot, and support audiovisual equipment including projectors, displays, microphones, speakers, cameras, control systems, and collaboration technologies. Support classroom instruction, webinars, hybrid meetings, livestreams, and other multimedia initiatives. Perform routine classroom technology inspections and coordinate equipment repairs, maintenance, and vendor service as needed. Assist with multimedia documentation, user training, and technology best practices. IT Asset Management & Technology Maintain accurate inventory records for university-owned technology assets using approved asset management and IT service management systems. Assist with hardware and software deployments, technology refresh initiatives, lifecycle replacement, asset audits, inventory reconciliation, and equipment retirement following policies and procedures. Support software implementation, asset tracking, procurement coordination, receiving, staging, tagging, deployment, surplus processing, and inventory reporting. Ensure asset records, inventory documentation, and related systems remain accurate, complete, and current. Secondary Duties and Responsibilities* Participate in technology projects, campus deployments, operational initiatives, and continuous service improvement efforts. Collaborate with Information Technology teams, campus departments, vendors, and student employees to support daily operations and strategic technology initiatives. Develop and maintain technical documentation, knowledge base articles, procedures, and operational resources. Participate in professional development activities such as remaining current with emerging technologies, industry best practices, and departmental training opportunities. Perform other duties as assigned. This position is generally scheduled to work Monday through Friday between 7:00 a.m. and 6:00 p.m. Regular, in-person attendance on Webster\u2019s campus is an essential function and physical requirement of the job. Work schedules are based on departmental and operational needs and may vary to support University operations. Occasional evening and weekend hours may be required to support classroom technology, IT-supported events, system implementations, maintenance activities, emergency response, and other operational requirements. This position is eligible for the University's Onsite Flex Work program in accordance with university policy and departmental operational requirements. Participation is based on job responsibilities, operational needs, and supervisory approval. *Any one position may not include all of the duties listed, nor do the examples necessarily include all of the duties performed. Functional Skills Can perform excellent written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills, including but not limited to; Teams, phone, email, in person, and the ticket management system. Demonstrates strong ticket management and communication skills by thoroughly documenting troubleshooting steps, actions taken, findings, and resolutions to ensure accurate case tracking, knowledge sharing, and seamless issue handoff when needed. Demonstrated commitment to providing exceptional customer service with professionalism, patience, and a customer-focused approach. Strong organizational, time management, and prioritization skills with the ability to manage multiple tasks and meet established deadlines. Ability to analyze problems, exercise sound judgment, and independently resolve technical issues while recognizing when escalation is appropriate. Ability to collaborate effectively within a team environment while also working independently with minimal supervision. Ability to adapt to changing technologies, priorities, and operational needs in a fast-paced environment. Ability to develop and maintain technical documentation, procedures, and knowledge resources. Ability to maintain confidentiality and safeguard sensitive information while complying with university policies and applicable regulations (e.g., FERPA, HIPAA, Title IX, and security awareness requirements). Technical Skills* Experience supporting Windows, MacOS, and mobile device operating systems. Knowledge of desktop, laptop, printer, peripheral, and audiovisual hardware installation, configuration, troubleshooting, and maintenance. Experience supporting classroom technology, conference room technology, multimedia systems, and collaboration platforms such as Microsoft Teams. Experience with endpoint management and device administration tools such as Microsoft Intune, JAMF, or similar platforms. Experience using IT service management and asset management systems to manage incidents, service requests, inventory, and technology assets. Knowledge of Microsoft 365 services, identity and access management, two-factor authentication, and basic account administration. Working knowledge of networking concepts, including TCP/IP, DNS, DHCP, wireless networking, VPN connectivity, and remote support technologies. Demonstrated troubleshooting and problem-solving skills across hardware, software, network, audiovisual, and endpoint technologies. Familiarity with software deployment, hardware lifecycle management, inventory control, and technology refresh processes. Understanding of cybersecurity best practices, endpoint security, phishing awareness, and data protection principles. Requisite Experience* Associate degree in Computer Science, Information Technology, or a related field, or an equivalent combination of education and relevant work experience. Experience providing technical support in an end-user computing, service desk, desktop support, or information technology environment. Experience supporting Windows, MacOS, mobile devices, and common business applications. Experience using an IT service management system to manage incidents, service requests, and technology assets. Experience supporting classroom technology, audiovisual systems, or event technology is preferred. Experience with IT asset management, inventory control, hardware deployments, or lifecycle management is preferred. Experience working in a higher education or similarly complex customer-focused environment is preferred."}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001232175600": {"title": "IT Support Technician", "description": "IT Support Technician. The IT Support Technician delivers comprehensive technical support and operational services to faculty, staff, students, and authorized affiliates across Webster University. As a member of the Infrastructure & Operations team, this role is responsible for providing end-user support, managing service desk operations, supporting classroom and audiovisual technologies, and assisting with IT asset management. The position plays a key role in ensuring the University\u2019s technology environment remains reliable, secure, and efficient. The IT Support Technician installs, configures, maintains, troubleshoots, and supports University-owned hardware, software, classroom technology, multimedia systems, and other IT-supported equipment while providing exceptional customer service through multiple service channels. This position also collaborates with Information Technology teams, campus departments, vendors, and student employees to support hardware and software deployments, asset lifecycle management, classroom instruction, IT-supported events, documentation, knowledge management, operational projects, and strategic technology initiatives across Webster University locations. Position-specific goals and objectives are established annually and align with the mission, vision, and strategic goals of Information Technology and Webster University. Essential Duties and Responsibilities* End User Support & Service Desk Respond to incidents, service requests, and technical support inquiries in accordance with established service level agreements (SLAs). Install, configure, maintain, troubleshoot, and support University-owned hardware, software, operating systems, mobile devices, printers, peripherals, and related technologies. Provide technical support through in-person, remote, telephone, email, and virtual support channels. This includes providing strong follow-up and claim ownership. Document, update, and maintain accurate records of incidents, service requests, assets, and work performed within the university's IT service management (ITSM) system. Diagnose and resolve hardware, software, network connectivity, account access, and other end-user technology issues, escalating requests when appropriate. Perform routine system maintenance, updates, equipment checks, and technology evaluations to ensure reliable service delivery. Classroom, AV, and Event Technology Support Provide technical support for classrooms, laboratories, conference rooms, and IT-supported events across Webster University locations. Install, configure, maintain, troubleshoot, and support audiovisual equipment including projectors, displays, microphones, speakers, cameras, control systems, and collaboration technologies. Support classroom instruction, webinars, hybrid meetings, livestreams, and other multimedia initiatives. Perform routine classroom technology inspections and coordinate equipment repairs, maintenance, and vendor service as needed. Assist with multimedia documentation, user training, and technology best practices. IT Asset Management & Technology Maintain accurate inventory records for university-owned technology assets using approved asset management and IT service management systems. Assist with hardware and software deployments, technology refresh initiatives, lifecycle replacement, asset audits, inventory reconciliation, and equipment retirement following policies and procedures. Support software implementation, asset tracking, procurement coordination, receiving, staging, tagging, deployment, surplus processing, and inventory reporting. Ensure asset records, inventory documentation, and related systems remain accurate, complete, and current. Secondary Duties and Responsibilities* Participate in technology projects, campus deployments, operational initiatives, and continuous service improvement efforts. Collaborate with Information Technology teams, campus departments, vendors, and student employees to support daily operations and strategic technology initiatives. Develop and maintain technical documentation, knowledge base articles, procedures, and operational resources. Participate in professional development activities such as remaining current with emerging technologies, industry best practices, and departmental training opportunities. Perform other duties as assigned. This position is generally scheduled to work Monday through Friday between 7:00 a.m. and 6:00 p.m. Regular, in-person attendance on Webster\u2019s campus is an essential function and physical requirement of the job. Work schedules are based on departmental and operational needs and may vary to support University operations. Occasional evening and weekend hours may be required to support classroom technology, IT-supported events, system implementations, maintenance activities, emergency response, and other operational requirements. This position is eligible for the University's Onsite Flex Work program in accordance with university policy and departmental operational requirements. Participation is based on job responsibilities, operational needs, and supervisory approval. *Any one position may not include all of the duties listed, nor do the examples necessarily include all of the duties performed. Functional Skills Can perform excellent written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills, including but not limited to; Teams, phone, email, in person, and the ticket management system. Demonstrates strong ticket management and communication skills by thoroughly documenting troubleshooting steps, actions taken, findings, and resolutions to ensure accurate case tracking, knowledge sharing, and seamless issue handoff when needed. Demonstrated commitment to providing exceptional customer service with professionalism, patience, and a customer-focused approach. Strong organizational, time management, and prioritization skills with the ability to manage multiple tasks and meet established deadlines. Ability to analyze problems, exercise sound judgment, and independently resolve technical issues while recognizing when escalation is appropriate. Ability to collaborate effectively within a team environment while also working independently with minimal supervision. Ability to adapt to changing technologies, priorities, and operational needs in a fast-paced environment. Ability to develop and maintain technical documentation, procedures, and knowledge resources. Ability to maintain confidentiality and safeguard sensitive information while complying with university policies and applicable regulations (e.g., FERPA, HIPAA, Title IX, and security awareness requirements). Technical Skills* Experience supporting Windows, MacOS, and mobile device operating systems. Knowledge of desktop, laptop, printer, peripheral, and audiovisual hardware installation, configuration, troubleshooting, and maintenance. Experience supporting classroom technology, conference room technology, multimedia systems, and collaboration platforms such as Microsoft Teams. Experience with endpoint management and device administration tools such as Microsoft Intune, JAMF, or similar platforms. Experience using IT service management and asset management systems to manage incidents, service requests, inventory, and technology assets. Knowledge of Microsoft 365 services, identity and access management, two-factor authentication, and basic account administration. Working knowledge of networking concepts, including TCP/IP, DNS, DHCP, wireless networking, VPN connectivity, and remote support technologies. Demonstrated troubleshooting and problem-solving skills across hardware, software, network, audiovisual, and endpoint technologies. Familiarity with software deployment, hardware lifecycle management, inventory control, and technology refresh processes. Understanding of cybersecurity best practices, endpoint security, phishing awareness, and data protection principles. Requisite Experience* Associate degree in Computer Science, Information Technology, or a related field, or an equivalent combination of education and relevant work experience. Experience providing technical support in an end-user computing, service desk, desktop support, or information technology environment. Experience supporting Windows, MacOS, mobile devices, and common business applications. Experience using an IT service management system to manage incidents, service requests, and technology assets. Experience supporting classroom technology, audiovisual systems, or event technology is preferred. Experience with IT asset management, inventory control, hardware deployments, or lifecycle management is preferred. Experience working in a higher education or similarly complex customer-focused environment is preferred."}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001232175100": {"title": "IT Support Technician", "description": "IT Support Technician. The IT Support Technician delivers comprehensive technical support and operational services to faculty, staff, students, and authorized affiliates across Webster University. As a member of the Infrastructure & Operations team, this role is responsible for providing end-user support, managing service desk operations, supporting classroom and audiovisual technologies, and assisting with IT asset management. The position plays a key role in ensuring the University\u2019s technology environment remains reliable, secure, and efficient. The IT Support Technician installs, configures, maintains, troubleshoots, and supports University-owned hardware, software, classroom technology, multimedia systems, and other IT-supported equipment while providing exceptional customer service through multiple service channels. This position also collaborates with Information Technology teams, campus departments, vendors, and student employees to support hardware and software deployments, asset lifecycle management, classroom instruction, IT-supported events, documentation, knowledge management, operational projects, and strategic technology initiatives across Webster University locations. Position-specific goals and objectives are established annually and align with the mission, vision, and strategic goals of Information Technology and Webster University. Essential Duties and Responsibilities* End User Support & Service Desk Respond to incidents, service requests, and technical support inquiries in accordance with established service level agreements (SLAs). Install, configure, maintain, troubleshoot, and support University-owned hardware, software, operating systems, mobile devices, printers, peripherals, and related technologies. Provide technical support through in-person, remote, telephone, email, and virtual support channels. This includes providing strong follow-up and claim ownership. Document, update, and maintain accurate records of incidents, service requests, assets, and work performed within the university's IT service management (ITSM) system. Diagnose and resolve hardware, software, network connectivity, account access, and other end-user technology issues, escalating requests when appropriate. Perform routine system maintenance, updates, equipment checks, and technology evaluations to ensure reliable service delivery. Classroom, AV, and Event Technology Support Provide technical support for classrooms, laboratories, conference rooms, and IT-supported events across Webster University locations. Install, configure, maintain, troubleshoot, and support audiovisual equipment including projectors, displays, microphones, speakers, cameras, control systems, and collaboration technologies. Support classroom instruction, webinars, hybrid meetings, livestreams, and other multimedia initiatives. Perform routine classroom technology inspections and coordinate equipment repairs, maintenance, and vendor service as needed. Assist with multimedia documentation, user training, and technology best practices. IT Asset Management & Technology Maintain accurate inventory records for university-owned technology assets using approved asset management and IT service management systems. Assist with hardware and software deployments, technology refresh initiatives, lifecycle replacement, asset audits, inventory reconciliation, and equipment retirement following policies and procedures. Support software implementation, asset tracking, procurement coordination, receiving, staging, tagging, deployment, surplus processing, and inventory reporting. Ensure asset records, inventory documentation, and related systems remain accurate, complete, and current. Secondary Duties and Responsibilities* Participate in technology projects, campus deployments, operational initiatives, and continuous service improvement efforts. Collaborate with Information Technology teams, campus departments, vendors, and student employees to support daily operations and strategic technology initiatives. Develop and maintain technical documentation, knowledge base articles, procedures, and operational resources. Participate in professional development activities such as remaining current with emerging technologies, industry best practices, and departmental training opportunities. Perform other duties as assigned. This position is generally scheduled to work Monday through Friday between 7:00 a.m. and 6:00 p.m. Regular, in-person attendance on Webster\u2019s campus is an essential function and physical requirement of the job. Work schedules are based on departmental and operational needs and may vary to support University operations. Occasional evening and weekend hours may be required to support classroom technology, IT-supported events, system implementations, maintenance activities, emergency response, and other operational requirements. This position is eligible for the University's Onsite Flex Work program in accordance with university policy and departmental operational requirements. Participation is based on job responsibilities, operational needs, and supervisory approval. *Any one position may not include all of the duties listed, nor do the examples necessarily include all of the duties performed. Functional Skills Can perform excellent written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills, including but not limited to; Teams, phone, email, in person, and the ticket management system. Demonstrates strong ticket management and communication skills by thoroughly documenting troubleshooting steps, actions taken, findings, and resolutions to ensure accurate case tracking, knowledge sharing, and seamless issue handoff when needed. Demonstrated commitment to providing exceptional customer service with professionalism, patience, and a customer-focused approach. Strong organizational, time management, and prioritization skills with the ability to manage multiple tasks and meet established deadlines. Ability to analyze problems, exercise sound judgment, and independently resolve technical issues while recognizing when escalation is appropriate. Ability to collaborate effectively within a team environment while also working independently with minimal supervision. Ability to adapt to changing technologies, priorities, and operational needs in a fast-paced environment. Ability to develop and maintain technical documentation, procedures, and knowledge resources. Ability to maintain confidentiality and safeguard sensitive information while complying with university policies and applicable regulations (e.g., FERPA, HIPAA, Title IX, and security awareness requirements). Technical Skills* Experience supporting Windows, MacOS, and mobile device operating systems. Knowledge of desktop, laptop, printer, peripheral, and audiovisual hardware installation, configuration, troubleshooting, and maintenance. Experience supporting classroom technology, conference room technology, multimedia systems, and collaboration platforms such as Microsoft Teams. Experience with endpoint management and device administration tools such as Microsoft Intune, JAMF, or similar platforms. Experience using IT service management and asset management systems to manage incidents, service requests, inventory, and technology assets. Knowledge of Microsoft 365 services, identity and access management, two-factor authentication, and basic account administration. Working knowledge of networking concepts, including TCP/IP, DNS, DHCP, wireless networking, VPN connectivity, and remote support technologies. Demonstrated troubleshooting and problem-solving skills across hardware, software, network, audiovisual, and endpoint technologies. Familiarity with software deployment, hardware lifecycle management, inventory control, and technology refresh processes. Understanding of cybersecurity best practices, endpoint security, phishing awareness, and data protection principles. Requisite Experience* Associate degree in Computer Science, Information Technology, or a related field, or an equivalent combination of education and relevant work experience. Experience providing technical support in an end-user computing, service desk, desktop support, or information technology environment. Experience supporting Windows, MacOS, mobile devices, and common business applications. Experience using an IT service management system to manage incidents, service requests, and technology assets. Experience supporting classroom technology, audiovisual systems, or event technology is preferred. Experience with IT asset management, inventory control, hardware deployments, or lifecycle management is preferred. Experience working in a higher education or similarly complex customer-focused environment is preferred."}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001232174806": {"title": "Sr. IT Support Lead", "description": "Sr. IT Support Lead. The Senior IT Support Lead serves as the senior technical resource for the IT Support team, delivering advanced technical support and operational leadership to faculty, staff, students, and authorized affiliates across Webster University. As a member of the Infrastructure & Operations team, this position provides advanced end-user support, service desk operations, classroom and audiovisual technology support, endpoint administration, and IT asset management while serving as the primary escalation point for complex technical issues and operational support activities. Essential Duties and Responsibilities* End User Support & Service Desk Respond to incidents, service requests, and technical support inquiries in accordance with established service level agreements (SLAs). Install, configure, maintain, troubleshoot, and support University-owned hardware, software, operating systems, mobile devices, printers, peripherals, and related technologies. Provide technical support through in-person, remote, telephone, email, and virtual support channels. This includes providing strong follow-up and claim ownership. Document, update, and maintain accurate records of incidents, service requests, assets, and work performed within the university's IT service management (ITSM) system. Diagnose and resolve hardware, software, network connectivity, account access, and other end-user technology issues, escalating requests when appropriate. Perform routine system maintenance, updates, equipment checks, and technology evaluations to ensure reliable service delivery. Classroom, AV, and Event Technology Support Serve as the primary technical escalation point for complex incidents and service requests. Provide technical support for classrooms, laboratories, conference rooms, and IT-supported events across Webster University locations. Install, configure, maintain, troubleshoot, and support audiovisual equipment including projectors, displays, microphones, speakers, cameras, control systems, and collaboration technologies. Support classroom instruction, webinars, hybrid meetings, livestreams, and other multimedia initiatives. Perform routine classroom technology inspections and coordinate equipment repairs, maintenance, and vendor service as needed. Assist with multimedia documentation, user training, and technology best practices. IT Asset Management & Technology Serve as the primary technical escalation point for complex incidents and service requests, providing advanced troubleshooting and quality assurance before escalating to other Information Technology teams. Maintain accurate inventory records for university-owned technology assets using approved asset management and IT service management systems. Support software implementation, asset tracking, procurement coordination, receiving, staging, tagging, deployment, surplus processing, and inventory reporting. Ensure asset records, inventory documentation, and related systems remain accurate, complete, and current. Independent Judgment and Decision-Making Exercise independent judgment and decision-making by carrying out process frameworks and adapting to real world scenarios. Lead or coordinate technology deployments, operating system upgrades, software rollouts, hardware refresh initiatives, and other campus technology projects. Serve as the primary technical escalation point for complex issues, and own escalations involving significant operational impacts. Mentor IT Support Technicians and student employees by providing technical guidance, knowledge sharing, and assistance with complex technical issues. Secondary Duties and Responsibilities* Serve as a secondary administrator for enterprise endpoint management platforms (e.g., Microsoft Intune, Ivanti, JAMF) by assisting with device administration, software deployments, policy testing, and endpoint management activities. Coordinate with vendors regarding equipment repairs, warranty claims, maintenance, break/fix services, product evaluations, and technical support requests. Test, evaluate, and validate new hardware, software, operating system updates, endpoint configurations, and other technologies before production deployment. Develop, review, and maintain technical documentation, knowledge base articles, standard operating procedures, and technical standards. Participate in lifecycle planning, deployment planning, technology evaluation, and other operational planning meetings while providing technical recommendations. Assist with hardware and software asset management activities, including inventory reconciliation, lifecycle tracking, procurement coordination, and technology deployment documentation. Participate in technology projects, continuous service improvement initiatives, and departmental professional development activities. Perform other duties as assigned. This position is generally scheduled to work Monday through Friday between 7:00 a.m. and 6:00 p.m. Regular, in-person attendance on Webster\u2019s campus is an essential function and physical requirement of the job. Work schedules are based on departmental and operational needs and may vary to support University operations. Occasional evening and weekend hours may be required to support classroom technology, IT-supported events, system implementations, maintenance activities, emergency response, and other operational requirements. This position is eligible for the University's Onsite Flex Work program in accordance with university policy and departmental operational requirements. Participation is based on job responsibilities, operational needs, and supervisory approval. *Any one position may not include all of the duties listed, nor do the examples necessarily include all of the duties performed. Functional Skills Can perform excellent written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills, including but not limited to; Teams, phone, email, in person, and the ticket management system. Demonstrates strong ticket management and communication skills by thoroughly documenting troubleshooting steps, actions taken, findings, and resolutions to ensure accurate case tracking, knowledge sharing, and seamless issue handoff when needed. Demonstrated commitment to providing exceptional customer service with professionalism, patience, and a customer-focused approach. Strong organizational, time management, and prioritization skills with the ability to manage multiple tasks and meet established deadlines. Ability to analyze problems, exercise sound judgment, and independently resolve technical issues while recognizing when escalation is appropriate. Ability to collaborate effectively within a team environment while also working independently with minimal supervision. Ability to adapt to changing technologies, priorities, and operational needs in a fast-paced environment. Ability to develop and maintain technical documentation, procedures, and knowledge resources. Ability to maintain confidentiality and safeguard sensitive information while complying with university policies and applicable regulations (e.g., FERPA, HIPAA, Title IX, and security awareness requirements). Requisite Experience* Bachelor's degree in Computer Science, Information Technology, Information Systems, or a related field preferred. Minimum of five (5) years of progressively responsible experience providing enterprise desktop support, service desk operations, endpoint management, classroom technology support, or related technical support services. Demonstrated experience serving as a technical lead, senior technician, or primary escalation point for complex technical issues. Experience working in a higher education or similarly complex customer-focused technology environment preferred. Experience providing advanced troubleshooting for Windows, macOS, Microsoft 365, endpoint management platforms, networking and cybersecurity basic fundamentals, audiovisual technologies, and enterprise business applications. Experience coordinating technology deployments, hardware refresh initiatives, software rollouts, operating system upgrades, or other campus technology projects. Experience working with endpoint management platforms such as Microsoft Intune, Ivanti, JAMF, or similar enterprise device management solutions. Experience coordinating with technology vendors regarding equipment repairs, warranty services, product evaluations, maintenance, and break/fix support. Experience developing and maintaining technical documentation, knowledge base articles, standard operating procedures, and technical standards. Experience supporting IT asset management activities, including hardware lifecycle management, inventory control, asset reconciliation, procurement coordination, and technology deployments. Experience testing, validating, and evaluating new hardware, software, operating system updates, endpoint policies, and enterprise technology solutions before production deployment."}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001232173706": {"title": "IT Support Technician", "description": "IT Support Technician. The IT Support Technician delivers comprehensive technical support and operational services to faculty, staff, students, and authorized affiliates across Webster University. As a member of the Infrastructure & Operations team, this role is responsible for providing end-user support, managing service desk operations, supporting classroom and audiovisual technologies, and assisting with IT asset management. The position plays a key role in ensuring the University\u2019s technology environment remains reliable, secure, and efficient. The IT Support Technician installs, configures, maintains, troubleshoots, and supports University-owned hardware, software, classroom technology, multimedia systems, and other IT-supported equipment while providing exceptional customer service through multiple service channels. This position also collaborates with Information Technology teams, campus departments, vendors, and student employees to support hardware and software deployments, asset lifecycle management, classroom instruction, IT-supported events, documentation, knowledge management, operational projects, and strategic technology initiatives across Webster University locations. Position-specific goals and objectives are established annually and align with the mission, vision, and strategic goals of Information Technology and Webster University. Essential Duties and Responsibilities* End User Support & Service Desk Respond to incidents, service requests, and technical support inquiries in accordance with established service level agreements (SLAs). Install, configure, maintain, troubleshoot, and support University-owned hardware, software, operating systems, mobile devices, printers, peripherals, and related technologies. Provide technical support through in-person, remote, telephone, email, and virtual support channels. This includes providing strong follow-up and claim ownership. Document, update, and maintain accurate records of incidents, service requests, assets, and work performed within the university's IT service management (ITSM) system. Diagnose and resolve hardware, software, network connectivity, account access, and other end-user technology issues, escalating requests when appropriate. Perform routine system maintenance, updates, equipment checks, and technology evaluations to ensure reliable service delivery. Classroom, AV, and Event Technology Support Provide technical support for classrooms, laboratories, conference rooms, and IT-supported events across Webster University locations. Install, configure, maintain, troubleshoot, and support audiovisual equipment including projectors, displays, microphones, speakers, cameras, control systems, and collaboration technologies. Support classroom instruction, webinars, hybrid meetings, livestreams, and other multimedia initiatives. Perform routine classroom technology inspections and coordinate equipment repairs, maintenance, and vendor service as needed. Assist with multimedia documentation, user training, and technology best practices. IT Asset Management & Technology Maintain accurate inventory records for university-owned technology assets using approved asset management and IT service management systems. Assist with hardware and software deployments, technology refresh initiatives, lifecycle replacement, asset audits, inventory reconciliation, and equipment retirement following policies and procedures. Support software implementation, asset tracking, procurement coordination, receiving, staging, tagging, deployment, surplus processing, and inventory reporting. Ensure asset records, inventory documentation, and related systems remain accurate, complete, and current. Secondary Duties and Responsibilities* Participate in technology projects, campus deployments, operational initiatives, and continuous service improvement efforts. Collaborate with Information Technology teams, campus departments, vendors, and student employees to support daily operations and strategic technology initiatives. Develop and maintain technical documentation, knowledge base articles, procedures, and operational resources. Participate in professional development activities such as remaining current with emerging technologies, industry best practices, and departmental training opportunities. Perform other duties as assigned. This position is generally scheduled to work Monday through Friday between 7:00 a.m. and 6:00 p.m. Regular, in-person attendance on Webster\u2019s campus is an essential function and physical requirement of the job. Work schedules are based on departmental and operational needs and may vary to support University operations. Occasional evening and weekend hours may be required to support classroom technology, IT-supported events, system implementations, maintenance activities, emergency response, and other operational requirements. This position is eligible for the University's Onsite Flex Work program in accordance with university policy and departmental operational requirements. Participation is based on job responsibilities, operational needs, and supervisory approval. *Any one position may not include all of the duties listed, nor do the examples necessarily include all of the duties performed. Functional Skills Can perform excellent written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills, including but not limited to; Teams, phone, email, in person, and the ticket management system. Demonstrates strong ticket management and communication skills by thoroughly documenting troubleshooting steps, actions taken, findings, and resolutions to ensure accurate case tracking, knowledge sharing, and seamless issue handoff when needed. Demonstrated commitment to providing exceptional customer service with professionalism, patience, and a customer-focused approach. Strong organizational, time management, and prioritization skills with the ability to manage multiple tasks and meet established deadlines. Ability to analyze problems, exercise sound judgment, and independently resolve technical issues while recognizing when escalation is appropriate. Ability to collaborate effectively within a team environment while also working independently with minimal supervision. Ability to adapt to changing technologies, priorities, and operational needs in a fast-paced environment. Ability to develop and maintain technical documentation, procedures, and knowledge resources. Ability to maintain confidentiality and safeguard sensitive information while complying with university policies and applicable regulations (e.g., FERPA, HIPAA, Title IX, and security awareness requirements). Technical Skills* Experience supporting Windows, MacOS, and mobile device operating systems. Knowledge of desktop, laptop, printer, peripheral, and audiovisual hardware installation, configuration, troubleshooting, and maintenance. Experience supporting classroom technology, conference room technology, multimedia systems, and collaboration platforms such as Microsoft Teams. Experience with endpoint management and device administration tools such as Microsoft Intune, JAMF, or similar platforms. Experience using IT service management and asset management systems to manage incidents, service requests, inventory, and technology assets. Knowledge of Microsoft 365 services, identity and access management, two-factor authentication, and basic account administration. Working knowledge of networking concepts, including TCP/IP, DNS, DHCP, wireless networking, VPN connectivity, and remote support technologies. Demonstrated troubleshooting and problem-solving skills across hardware, software, network, audiovisual, and endpoint technologies. Familiarity with software deployment, hardware lifecycle management, inventory control, and technology refresh processes. Understanding of cybersecurity best practices, endpoint security, phishing awareness, and data protection principles. Requisite Experience* Associate degree in Computer Science, Information Technology, or a related field, or an equivalent combination of education and relevant work experience. Experience providing technical support in an end-user computing, service desk, desktop support, or information technology environment. Experience supporting Windows, MacOS, mobile devices, and common business applications. Experience using an IT service management system to manage incidents, service requests, and technology assets. Experience supporting classroom technology, audiovisual systems, or event technology is preferred. Experience with IT asset management, inventory control, hardware deployments, or lifecycle management is preferred. Experience working in a higher education or similarly complex customer-focused environment is preferred."}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001220008406": {"title": "Learning Systems Support Lead", "description": "Learning Systems Support Lead. The Learning Systems Support Lead provides day-to-day technical and functional support for the University\u2019s learning management system (LMS) and related teaching and learning technologies. The Analyst supports faculty, students, and staff and serves as a primary point of contact for Instructional/Educational Technology-related support requests from the University IT Service Desk and other University support teams. Support requests are received through the University\u2019s service management system, OLC Support email, telephone, and referrals from other University offices. The Analyst monitors and manages this incoming support activity; troubleshoots and resolves technical and administrative issues; and triages, escalates, coordinates, and follows up on issues requiring assistance from LMS Administration, Information Technology, Enrollment, Instructional Design, or other functional and technical areas. The Analyst also serves as the backup LMS Administrator. In addition to providing direct support, the Learning Systems Support Lead serves as the primary operational owner of learning systems support function, including processes, workflows, escalation pathways, documentation, and self-help resources. The Lead analyzes support activity to identify recurring issues and trends, investigates root causes, maintains support processes and self-help resources, and coordinates with appropriate University offices to address recurring issues and improve the effectiveness of the support model. The Analyst also assists with course maintenance and other learning systems responsibilities as needed. Reports to the Director, Online Learning Center. Essential Duties and Responsibilities* Provide day-to-day technical and functional support for the LMS and related teaching and learning technologies, including resolving technical and administrative issues for faculty, students, and staff. Provide Instructional/Educational Technology-related support to the University's Information Technology Service Desk and other University support teams, troubleshooting issues and coordinating escalation or resolution with appropriate functional and technical areas. Manage the day-to-day learning systems support operation, including incoming requests received through the University's service management system, OLC Support email, telephone, and other support channels; assess and prioritize requests and resolve, triage, escalate, coordinate, and follow up on issues through appropriate resolution. Participate in live support activities via video conferencing and chat as necessary and required. Analyze support activity and service data to identify recurring issues, trends, and opportunities for improvement; lead investigation of root causes and coordinate cross-functional resolution of systemic or recurring support issues with appropriate University offices. Develop, maintain, and improve support documentation, FAQs, self-help resources, standard procedures, and other knowledge resources for faculty, students, staff, and University support teams. Establish, maintain, and continuously improve learning systems support processes, workflows, service practices, communications, escalation pathways, and other resources to ensure effective and consistent support and reduce recurring support needs. Build and maintain course content in Canvas using the Canvas Rich Content Editor and HTML/CSS as appropriate. Support the Instructional Design team in the maintenance of online courses, including updating course content and resolving content-related technical issues. Collaborate with faculty to make minor course content updates, including correcting broken links, formatting pages, linking files, and addressing other course content issues. Serve as backup LMS Administrator and assist with LMS administration and learning systems operations as needed. Participate in after-hours support as required to maintain University responsiveness to user support issues. Experience using and navigating a learning management system (LMS), preferably Canvas. Experience using Microsoft 365 and common teaching and learning technologies. Demonstrated technical troubleshooting, analytical, and problem-solving skills, including the ability to investigate issues, identify potential root causes, and determine appropriate next steps. Ability to recognize patterns and recurring issues across support requests and identify opportunities for process, system, documentation, or support improvements. Excellent customer service and communication skills, including the ability to interpret and communicate technical information clearly and effectively to users with varying levels of technical expertise. Ability to develop clear, accurate, and user-friendly technical documentation, procedures, FAQs, and self-help resources. Strong organizational skills and attention to detail, with the ability to prioritize and manage multiple support requests, issues, and responsibilities. Ability to learn new systems and technologies effectively and independently. Demonstrated ability to work productively across functional and technical teams and coordinate the resolution of issues involving multiple stakeholders. Ability to work independently, exercise sound judgment, and take ownership of issues through appropriate resolution, escalation, or coordination. Demonstrated ability to lead or coordinate a technical support function, workflow, or service, including establishing priorities, coordinating work across stakeholders, and improving support processes. 3-5 years of progressively responsible experience supporting a learning management system, enterprise application, instructional technology environment, or related technical support function. Experience coordinating technical support workflows, service operations, or cross-functional issue resolution preferred. Bachelor's degree in informational technology, instructional technology, education, business, or related field required. An equivalent combination of education and substantial progressively responsible relevant experience may be considered."}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001231156000": {"title": "Electronic Resources Librarian", "description": "Electronic Resources Librarian. General Statement of Duties: The Electronic Resources Librarian is responsible for ensuring reliable access to the library's electronic collections and online services. The position manages the lifecycle of electronic resources, serves as the primary contact for access and troubleshooting issues, and works collaboratively with vendors, library staff, consortia partners, and university IT to resolve resource access problems. The librarian plays a key role in maintaining and improving the systems that support resource discovery and delivery, including the integrated library system, authentication services, discovery tools, and related technologies. This position contributes to the implementation and optimization of library systems and helps develop efficient, user-centered workflows that improve access to information resources. Responsibilities draw heavily from the library's current electronic resources management functions while maintaining a meaningful role in systems administration and migration activities. Essential Duties and Responsibilities* Electronic Resources Access, Troubleshooting, and Management (55%) Manages the lifecycle of electronic resources, including evaluation, ordering, implementation, activation, maintenance, renewal support, payments, and cancellation. Serves as the library's primary contact for electronic resource access issues and troubleshooting. Monitors and responds to reports of problems affecting databases, eBooks, e-journals, streaming media, and other online resources. Investigates and resolves access problems in collaboration with vendors, University IT, and other partners. Maintains high service standards for responding to electronic resource issues and communicating status updates to stakeholders. Maintains documentation related to electronic resources, licensing terms, administrative credentials, invoicing, and workflows. Tracks vendor communications regarding content changes, platform changes, outages, and service disruptions. Monitors availability and performance of electronic resources; proactively identifies and resolves potential issues. Supports management of the library's materials and membership budget lines. Each year analyzes spending patterns and as a member of the Library Budget Task Force, participates in developing a financial forecast to guide the library's budget request. Reviews financial records for selected library materials appropriations and expenditures. Reconciles account balances between the library's records and Webster's accounting system. Regularly communicates with library's Management Team and subject librarians regarding budget position; ensures that funds are expended appropriately and in a timely manner. Works closely with the finance department to ensure that the library's financial processes adhere to Webster and accounting best practices. Leads implementation of financial process changes in the library. Reviews invoices and submits purchase requisitions and invoices through Webster's accounting systems. Maintains accurate electronic resource records within Library systems. Library System Administration and Technology (25%) Serves as a library systems administrator for the integrated library system and related applications. Serves as a co-lead on system implementation, migration, testing, configuration, and ongoing maintenance activities. Maintains authentication services, proxy configuration, discovery services, and other tools supporting access to library resources. Collaborates with library staff to improve workflows through effective use of library technologies. Maintains documentation for system configuration and procedures. Evaluates emerging technologies and recommends improvements that enhance access and operational efficiency. Assessment, Reporting, and Data Management (10%) Collects, analyzes, and reports usage statistics for electronic resources. Develops reports and dashboards that support collection and operational decision making. Coordinates collection assessment and resource review projects. Maintains the databases that feed the library\u2019s Publication Finder and link resolver; adds, modifies, or deletes resources from these databases as appropriate. Participates in data cleanup, migration, and quality-control initiatives. Provides data and analysis to support strategic planning and collection management decisions. Liaison and Professional Responsibilities (10%) Serves as liaison to one or more academic departments. Participates in collection development activities for assigned subject areas. Provides research consultations and instructional support as appropriate. Serves on library, university, consortium, and professional committees. Engages in professional development and remains current with developments in electronic resources, library systems, and academic librarianship. Secondary Duties and Responsibilities Serves on Library and University committees as appropriate Works at service desk as staff back-up when needed May supervise student employees and coordinate staff assigned to projects related to electronic resources, systems and data management. Perform other duties as assigned. Master's degree in Library and Information Science from an ALA-accredited institution or equivalent. Experience managing or supporting electronic resources in a library environment. Experience troubleshooting electronic resource access issues. Strong customer service orientation and commitment to responsive problem solving. Demonstrated ability to communicate effectively with vendors, colleagues, and end users. Strong organizational, analytical, and project management skills. Ability to prioritize multiple responsibilities and adapt to changing needs. Ability to work independently and collaboratively. Minimum of three-years of library experience or comparable experience"}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001227908806": {"title": "Adjunct Instructor\u2014Stage Management Instructor/Mentor", "description": "Adjunct Instructor\u2014Stage Management Instructor/Mentor. Webster University\u2019s Sargent Conservatory of Theatre Arts invites applications for the position of Stage Management Instructor/Mentor for Stage Management majors. Essential Duties Teach Stage Management 1 Teach Stage Management Topics: Non-Traditional Work Teach 4 th year Professional Practice Teach Stage Management Seminar Mentor student stage managers on four Conservatory productions and one production for the Dance program Host prospective students in classes and on campus as able; join prospective interviews with other production faculty as schedule permits Bachelor's degree and/or extensive experience in the field"}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001200918506": {"title": "Undergraduate Admissions Counselor", "description": "Undergraduate Admissions Counselor. The Undergraduate Admissions Counselor identifies, recruits, and contributes to increasing the population of admitted and enrolled undergraduate students (freshmen and transfer) and is responsible for program development and implementation. The Counselor must be familiar with successful recruitment practices and must have a naturally enthusiastic and persuasive manner to recruit and convert undergraduate students to enroll at Webster University. Additional responsibility for this position will include admissions-related file processing and supporting the admissions operations to increase undergraduate students at Webster USA campuses. This position reports to the Associate Director, Admissions. Essential Duties and Responsibilities* \u200b \u200bRecruitment/Territory Management \u2013 35% \u200bRecruits new freshmen and/or transfer students to Webster University and assists with recruiting plans and events both on and off campus. \u200bManages an assigned geographic territory and travels to high schools, community colleges, community-based organizations; attend college fairs and conduct special programs across the country. \u200bPlan all aspects of travel, manage receipts, and prepare travel reports to meet policies and deadlines. \u200bRepresents undergraduate admissions in a public-speaking capacity both on and off campus and maintains records of these activities in SLATE. Admissions/Advising \u2013 50% \u200bResponsible for counseling and outreach to students and parents on the admissions requirements and procedures, and works directly with them to resolve extenuating circumstances, complaints and appeals \u200bManages data, prospects, applicants, and recruitment territories through the SLATE (CRM) system to enhance student relationships, communication timelines and to connect students with other areas of the university. \u200bCultivate student applicant pool and assist with the transition to campus for incoming students \u200bIn accordance with university guidelines and enrollment objectives, determines eligibility of applicants for admissions based on analysis of the admission application, transcripts, standardized tests, and supplemental documentation. \u200bUtilizes professional judgement to analyze the complete student file to determine full admission, admission by exception, denial or to send the application on for further review. \u200bResponds, within established time benchmarks and service delivery protocols, to inquiries regarding admissions status from prospective students. \u200bProvides assistance to prospective students regarding admissions requirements and process. \u200bUpdates information in SLATE and other university systems. \u200bMaintains a high level of working knowledge of university information, organization, opportunities, and policies \u200bResponsible for advising and counseling special populations that may include but are not limited to athletes, rural students, urban students, conservatory, honors, competitive scholars, etc. \u200bProgramming \u2013 10% \u200bAssists and participates in a variety of on-campus and off-campus recruitment and yield programs, virtually and in person \u200bImplements recruitment activities such as application workshops, information sessions, financial aid information sessions and outreach presentations. Secondary Duties and Responsibilities* \u200b \u200bOther duties and Assignments \u2013 5% \u200bCultivates working relationships with university schools and colleges, departments, counselors and advisors to enhance student service and satisfaction as well as establish community contacts \u200bReports statistical data on programs, projects, SLATE cases and travel activities \u200bServes on internal committees and task forces and participates in work groups to support the mission of Enrollment Management and Webster University QUALIFICATIONS FOR APPOINTMENT Functional/Technical Skills* \u200b \u200bSkill in operating a computer and supporting software \u200b\u200bKnowledge of immigration rules and regulations. \u200bKnowledge of college admissions policies and procedures. \u200bSkill in reviewing/evaluating transcripts. \u200bStrong interpersonal communication skills. \u200bAbility to demonstrate cultural sensitivity and diplomacy. \u200bAbility to effectively communicate in oral and written form. \u200bAbility to learn and apply applicable federal regulations. \u200bAbility to work independently without direct supervision. \u200bA working knowledge of higher education within the United States. Requisite Experience* One year of experience in recruitment, admissions, advising, or related enrollment and/or student service experience. Preferred Experience in higher education This position is eligible to advance to Sr. Undergraduate Admissions Counselor position after 2 years at counselor level and based on meeting performance expectations. Standard Schedule: \u200bDays 8:30am \u2013 4:30pm with occasional evening and weekend hours. \u200b If appropriate indicate if work is fully on-site. If under a Flexible/Remote Work Arrangement, add \u201cThis position is approved for a <enter specific arrangement: compressed work week, on-site flexible, 1- or 2- day hybrid, or fully remote> work arrangement as offered in Webster University\u2019s Flexible and/or Remote Work Arrangements policy. Use by individual employees is at the discretion of the supervisor and unit leadership based on department and University needs\u201d. *Any one position may not include all of the duties listed, nor do the examples necessarily include all of the duties performed \u200b Bachelor's Degree and one year of experience in recruitment, admissions, advising, or related enrollment and/or student service experience."}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001227036006": {"title": "Director, Graduate Recruitment & Admissions", "description": "Director, Graduate Recruitment & Admissions. General Statement of Duties : The Director, Graduate Enrollment provides operational leadership for graduate recruitment, admissions decision-making, and enrollment across Webster University's St. Louis campus, San Antonio campus, and U.S. domestic online programs. Reporting to the Associate Vice President for Strategic Enrollment Management/SIO, the Director is responsible for executing enrollment strategies that achieve institutional enrollment and revenue goals while ensuring timely, consistent, and student-centered admissions decisions. This is a highly tactical, performance-oriented leadership position. The Director leads graduate admissions counselors, oversees admissions file review and decision processes, partners with the admissions processing team, manages enrollment performance in real time, and implements data-driven and AI-enabled solutions to improve engagement and conversion throughout the enrollment funnel. Enrollment Leadership - 20% Execute graduate enrollment strategies aligned with institutional goals and revenue targets. Develop annual, term, and weekly recruitment plans and lead forecasting and enrollment planning. Monitor performance and implement corrective strategies to achieve enrollment goals. Recruitment Operations - 15% Direct graduate recruitment activities for St. Louis, San Antonio, and online domestic programs. Set recruitment priorities, counselor assignments, event strategy, and outreach expectations. Oversee graduate recruitment events, virtual sessions, campus visits, and appropriate travel. Admissions Decision & processing - 20% Serve as a senior admissions decision-maker, reviewing completed graduate application files and rendering admissions decisions in accordance with University policy. Review complex, borderline, and exception cases; exercise professional judgment and ensure consistency in admissions decisions. Partner with the admissions processing team to establish file-review priorities, resolve documentation issues, and improve application-to-decision turnaround times. Enrollment Funnel, AI & Engagement - 20% Own performance across the graduate enrollment funnel and analyze conversion trends, stalled populations, and operational barriers. Identify, implement, and manage relevant AI-enabled recruitment and engagement solutions to support lead prioritization, personalized outreach, application completion, yield, and anti-melt efforts. Evaluate AI and automation effectiveness and integrate solutions appropriately with CRM workflows and counselor engagement strategies. Team Leadership - 10% Supervise, coach, and develop graduate admissions counselors and establish clear performance expectations and accountability. Conduct regular pipeline, productivity, and admissions quality reviews and intervene when performance falls below expectations. Data, CRM, Analytics & Collaboration - 15% Utilize CRM, enrollment technologies, dashboards, and forecasts to guide decisions and monitor counselor productivity and data quality. Partner with Enrollment Technology, academic leadership, marketing, financial aid, admissions processing, operations, and student success. Support new program launches, market expansion, and continuous improvement of graduate enrollment processes. Five or more years of enrollment management or admissions leadership experience. Bachelor\u2019s Degree required. Preference: Graduate Enrollment/Recruitment/Admissions Functional/Technical Skills* Demonstrated success leading graduate recruitment and admissions teams. Experience reviewing graduate application files and making or overseeing admissions decisions. Ability to exercise professional judgment in complex, exception, and non-routine admissions cases. Strong leadership, analytical, communication, organizational, and time-management skills. Experience with CRM and enrollment technologies; Slate experience preferred. Ability to evaluate and implement AI, automation, and digital engagement solutions in an enrollment environment. Ability to use data and technology to improve operational efficiency, student engagement, and enrollment outcomes."}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001215314106": {"title": "Community Relations Coordinator", "description": "Community Relations Coordinator. The Community Relations Coordinator promotes community awareness of the Community Music School of Webster University among current and prospective students, parents, educators, key influencers for potential students, and potential donors. Markets and recruits students for all CMS programs. Manages the CMS website, social media accounts, and print and digital marketing materials. Coordinates all marketing efforts in conjunction with key administrators and the Webster Global Marketing Communications and LGCFA office. Reports to the CMS Director. Essential Duties and Responsibilities Serves as marketing liaison among the Community Music School (CMS), Webster University, and the community at large. Raises visibility in the region for the CMS among potential donors and key influencers for potential students. Monitors and updates the CMS website and social media accounts. Develops and monitors marketing and advertising plan with CMS Director. Designs, produces, and distributes all CMS digital and print materials. Communicates with constituents via email using Emma email system. Creates and distributes monthly e- newsletters. Informs local media of CMS events, programs and student/faculty accomplishments. Produces publications of the Community Music School with CMS staff for copy, graphic designers, printers, and mailing sources. Coordinates photo sessions for publications and digital marketing. Represents the School at targeted conferences and events. Creates and updates displays. Collects, monitors, and evaluates data regarding past, current, and prospective students and parents to inform marketing practices. Monitors and updates mailing and email lists. Assists with planning and implementing special events. Develops plans to connect with alumni and promote alumni programs. Provides information about the CMS programs to libraries, schools, clubs, and relevant venues Monitors trends in music education and related activities. Prepares student enrollment reports, NASM enrollment report and organization data. Secondary Duties and Responsibilities Represents CMS at 2-3 summer recruitment fairs per year. Hosts some School Recitals and coordinates annual Faculty Showcase. Monday \u2013 Friday, 8:30 a.m. \u2013 4:30 p.m. remote position; occasional on-site and weekend work required. Requisite Experience Minimum of 5 years in community relations, marketing, recruitment and/or project management. Functional/technical Skills Bachelor\u2019s degree or equivalent experience required. Strong organizational skills and effective interpersonal skills. Able to communicate effectively, both orally and in writing. Must be competent with Excel, Microsoft Word, Office 365, Teams, student management systems, websites, and social media. Experienced with Constant Contact, Emma, or similar email systems. Ability to exercise important human relations skills in establishing and maintaining effective working relations with the University and community."}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001225820606": {"title": "Guest Director for Sargent Conservatory of Theatre Arts\u2014Carrie: The Musical", "description": "Guest Director for Sargent Conservatory of Theatre Arts\u2014Carrie: The Musical. Webster University\u2019s Sargent Conservatory of Theatre Arts invites applications for the position of Guest D irector for Sargent Conservatory of Theatre Arts\u2019 Spring P roduction of CARRIE: THE MUSICAL Directors set their artistic vision for a show , including selecting the student cast, collaborating with the team of student designers, leading rehearsals, blocking /choreographing the action, and guiding the overall aesthetic of the production . Guest directors will: - Attend semester auditions to select the student cast members - Lead rehearsals - Monitor production's pacing - Collaborate with production team, including student stage managers - Collaborate with team of student designers - Collaborate with producer and faculty to ensure artistic process aligns with educational goals of the department - Deliver a high-caliber production Bachelor's degree and/or extensive experience in the field"}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001216751206": {"title": "Sr. HR Partner: Employee Relations & Institutional Compliance", "description": "Sr. HR Partner: Employee Relations & Institutional Compliance. The Senior HR Partner, Employee Relations & Institutional Compliance serves as a senior individual contributor responsible for providing advanced consultation and independently managing complex employee relations, civil rights, workplace investigation, accommodation, and institutional compliance matters. The Senior HR Partner exercises significant independent judgment in managing assigned matters, advising leaders, conducting investigations, identifying organizational risk, and developing practical recommendations. Complex, high-risk, precedent-setting, or institutionally significant matters are escalated to the Director, who retains functional ownership, program oversight, and decision authority. Essential Duties and Responsibilities 1. Employee Relations & Leader Consultation \u2013 30% Provide advanced consultation to leaders regarding employee relations, performance management, corrective action, workplace conflict, employee complaints, policy application, and other workplace matters. Independently assess complex employee concerns and recommend appropriate courses of action consistent with University policies and practices. Coach leaders on effective issue resolution, documentation, performance expectations, and employee communication. Support employee relations matters associated with organizational and workforce changes. Identify recurring trends, organizational risks, and opportunities for proactive intervention. 2. Civil Rights Compliance & Workplace Investigations \u2013 25% Conduct prompt, thorough, impartial, and appropriately documented investigations involving discrimination, harassment, retaliation, sexual misconduct, workplace misconduct, and other policy concerns. Conduct investigative planning; complainant, respondent, and witness interviews; gathering testimonial, documentary and electronic evidence; trauma-informed investigative practices; and preparation of comprehensive investigative reports. Support matters arising under Title VI, Title VII, Title IX, and other applicable civil rights and employment requirements. Maintain appropriate neutrality, discretion, confidentiality, and professional judgment when handling sensitive matters. Escalate complex or significant institutional risks to the Director/Title IX Coordinator. 3. ADA & Workplace Accommodations \u2013 20% Administer assigned workplace accommodation matters, including intake, documentation, interactive process discussions, case management, and follow-up. Consult with employees and leaders regarding essential job functions, workplace limitations, potential accommodations, and operational considerations. Develop accommodation recommendations consistent with established University procedures and applicable requirements. Maintain appropriate confidentiality of medical and accommodation information. Escalate complex, high-risk, disputed, or potentially precedent-setting accommodation matters to the Director. 4. Case Management, Compliance & Risk \u2013 15% Manage assigned employee relations, civil rights, investigation, and accommodation cases from intake through resolution. Maintain accurate, timely, and confidential case documentation and monitor applicable deadlines and follow-up requirements. Apply established employment and institutional policies consistently and identify matters requiring policy interpretation or escalation. Analyze case trends and data to identify systemic concerns, emerging risks, and opportunities for process improvement. Support compliance assessments, reporting, audits, and other institutional risk-management activities as assigned. 5. Education & Institutional Partnership \u2013 10% Develop and deliver training and guidance related to employee relations, civil rights, workplace conduct, investigations, accommodations, reporting responsibilities, and related compliance matters. Build effective partnerships across Human Resources, Academic Affairs, Student Affairs, Legal Affairs, Public Safety, and other University functions. Participate in institutional committees, working groups, and initiatives related to employee relations, civil rights, compliance, and workplace effectiveness. Support policy review, process improvement, and prevention initiatives. Perform other duties consistent with the scope and level of the position. Requisite Experience Seven to ten years of progressively responsible experience in employee relations, workplace investigations, civil rights compliance, Title IX, Human Resources, employment compliance, or a related field. Demonstrated experience independently managing complex civil rights investigations and sensitive employee relations investigations. Experience advising leaders on performance management, corrective action, workplace conflict, policy interpretation, and employee concerns. Bachelor\u2019s degree in Human Resources, Business Administration, Legal Studies, Organizational Leadership, Higher Education Administration, Psychology, or a related field required. Functional & Professional Competencies Advanced employee relations consultation and problem-solving. Workplace and civil rights investigation expertise. Working knowledge of Title VI, Title VII, Title IX, ADA, FMLA, and related employment and civil rights requirements. Strong case management and documentation capabilities. Ability to assess complex situations, identify risks, and develop practical recommendations. Strong consultation, facilitation, conflict-resolution, and relationship-management skills. Ability to influence leaders and stakeholders without formal authority. Strong written, verbal, analytical, and organizational skills. Demonstrated judgment, impartiality, discretion, and professionalism when handling sensitive matters. Ability to recognize matters requiring escalation based on legal, institutional, reputational, or operational risk"}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001218156206": {"title": "Assistant Baseball Coach", "description": "Assistant Baseball Coach. General Statement of Duties: The assistant coach provides instructional, administrative, and recruiting support services under the direction of the Head Coach and Director of Athletics. Essential Duties and Responsibilities* Assists the Head Coach in the implementation of sport training through teaching and instructing the student-athletes in the rules and fundamentals of baseball. Under the direction of the Head Coach, assists in providing sport skill instruction, assists in the conduct of practice sessions, performs game related duties, and motivates players to give their best efforts. Contributes to recruiting efforts through on-campus visits and off-campus evaluation. Supports team fundraising initiatives. Mentors student athletes. Demonstrates sincere interest in the personal, academic and athletic success of the student-athlete. Represents the University in a positive manner at all times. Abides by all SLIAC and NCAA rules and regulations. Secondary Duties and Responsibilities* Assisting other coaches in the department with game management duties. May supervise student assistants and/or student managers. Functional/Technical Skills* Demonstrated baseball coaching knowledge. Excellent interpersonal, teaching and communication skills. General knowledge of NCAA Division III rules. Bachelor\u2019s Degree from an accredited college or university. Minimum one (1) year of experience in coaching baseball or other related baseball activities. Strong communications skills, both written and verbal, and interpersonal skills. Strong organizational skills. Ability to work on multiple projects simultaneously. Ability to exercise important human relations skills in establishing and maintaining effective working relations with the university community and the public. Ability to maintain composure when faced with difficult situations and to effectively identify and resolve problems and to maintain strict confidentiality related to sensitive information. Demonstrated commitment to diversity and experience promoting inclusive excellence."}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001187575106": {"title": "Instructional Media Specialist", "description": "Instructional Media Specialist. Description of Work The Instructional Media Specialist is responsible for the ownership, operation, and continuous improvement of the university\u2019s academic video production and instructional media ecosystem , ensuring that video functions as a reliable, accessible, and pedagogically sound core teaching medium across Webster University\u2019s online and continuing education digital learning initiatives. This role combines academic media platform stewardship , high-quality instructional media production , faculty development and support , and accessibility accountability . The position is embedded in the course and continuing education development and revision lifecycle and serves as a required dependency for credit-bearing instruction, faculty training and support, and institutional digital learning initiatives . The Instructional Media Specialist plans, directs, shoots, edits, and produces professional instructional media, including documentary-style field productions, instructional demonstrations, interview recordings, faculty-led instructional video with integrated teaching graphics, and motion-graphics - based instructional media . The role serves as the primary steward of the university\u2019s academic video platform (Kaltura) , overseeing media organization, delivery, integration with Canvas, and distribution within course and continuing education learning environments. The position ensures all instructional video meets accessibility standards and supports faculty in the effective design, creation, and delivery of instructional media. The role requires a blend of creative production expertise and technical leadership , inc luding motion graphics, audio production, instructional video design, platform management, faculty consultation, and accessibility best practices. Essential Duties and Responsibilities Serve as the primary owner and steward of the university\u2019s academic video platform (Kaltura) , in cluding configuration, organization, permissions, integration with Canvas, and media distribution within course environments. Ensure the reliable, accessible, and pedagogically appropriate use of instructional video across online and continuing education courses, faculty training, and institutional digital learning initiatives. Plan, direct, shoot, edit, and publish high-quality instructional media in sup port of online and continuing education instruction and academic programs, including documentary-style field productions, instructional demonstrations, interviews, faculty-led instructional videos, and motion-graphics\u2013based instructional media. Collaborate with faculty, instructional designers, and academic units to design, produce, and integrate instructional media aligned with course outcomes, teaching strategies, and accessibility standards. Provid e faculty development and consultation rel ated to instructional video design, production practices, and effective use of media within Canvas courses. Ensure instructional media accessibility compliance, including oversight of captions, transcripts, audio descriptions, and remediation workflows for instructional video content, in alignment with academic accessibility standards and regulatory requirements . Produce and mix high-quality audio for instructional media, including voiceovers and live recordings, ensuring clarity and instructional effectiveness. Create instructional motion graphics and visual teaching assets, independently or in collaboration with designers. Monitor platform updates, emerging tools, and instructional media practices, and make institutional recommendations to improve academic media workflows, standards, and system use. Troubleshoot instructional video playback, embedding, and integration issues within learning management systems and academic platforms. Participate in onlin e and continuing education course development, redesign, content development , and cross-functional design sprints as a required media production and platform dependency . Contribute to the continuous improvement of instructional media standards, workflows, and visual communication practices in support of online learning. Perform other duties as assigned in support of academic instruction, faculty development, and digital learning initiatives . This is a part-time position located at our Webster Groves, Missouri campus. Qualifications For Appointment Ability to own and operate an academic instructional media ecosystem , including video production workflows, platform use, and media delivery within learning environments. Demonstrated proficiency in instructional video production , including camera operation, lighting, audio capture, editing, and post-production. Strong skills in instructional motion graphics and visual explanation , including the use of animated text, graphics, and teaching visuals to support learning outcomes. Working knowledge of video accessibility principles , including captions, transcripts, and audio descriptions, and the ability to apply accessibility best practices to instructional media. Ability to manage multiple concurrent projects , balancing long-term course development timelines with time-sensitive instructional and training needs. Strong interpersonal and communication skills , including the ability to collaborate with faculty, instructional designers, and non-technical stakeholders in both in-person and virtual environments. Self-directed with strong problem-solving skills , sound judgment, and a high degree of personal accountability. Collaborative mindset with the ability to contribute effectively to cross-functional academic and digital learning teams . Demonstrated commitment to continuous learning and adapting to evolving instructional media tools, platforms, and technologies. Please include a link to a professional reel or representative instructional media work as part of the application. Requisite Experience \u200b \u200b Bachelor\u2019s degree in film production, video production, media arts, or related field, or equivalent professional experienc e . \u200b Minimum of two years of professional instructional or media production experience . \u200b Experience planning, directing, and shooting field-based and studio-based video productions using DSLR or comparable camera systems. \u200b Experience with location and studio audio recording , including boom and lavalier microphone techniques. \u200b Professional experience using Adobe Creative Suite , including Premiere Pro and After Effects; experience with Illustrator and Photoshop for instructional media assets. \u200b Experience producing motion graphics and post-production visual elements , including green screen workflows. \u200b Experience preparing, transcoding, publishing, and archiving video for web-based and platform-based delivery . \u200b Familiarity with basic web publishing concepts , including HTML and CSS, as they relate to media embedding and delivery. \u200b Experience working with a range of standard software tools, including productivity applications, browsers, and media platforms."}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001221163406": {"title": "Assistant Volleyball Coach - Seasonal", "description": "Assistant Volleyball Coach - Seasonal. Primary duty is to assist the head coach in the implementation of sport training through teaching and instructing the student-athletes in the rules and fundamentals of volleyball. Under the direction of the head coach, will assist in providing sport skill instruction, assist in the conduct of practice sessions, perform game related duties, and motivate players to give their best efforts. Contribute to recruiting efforts through on-campus visits and off-campus evaluation. Support team fundraising initiatives. Mentor student athletes. Demonstrate sincere interest in the personal, academic and athletic success of the student-athlete. Represent the University in a positive manner at all times. Abide by all SLIAC and NCAA rules and regulations. Secondary Duties and Responsibilities* Assisting other coaches in the department with game management duties. May supervise student assistants and/or student managers. Functional/Technical Skills* Demonstrated volleyball coaching knowledge. Excellent interpersonal, teaching and communication skills. General knowledge of NCAA Division III rules. Bachelor\u2019s Degree from an accredited college or university. Minimum one (1) year of experience in coaching volleyball or other related volleyball activities. Strong communications skills, both written and verbal, and interpersonal skills. Strong organizational skills. Ability to work on multiple projects simultaneously. Ability to exercise important human relations skills in establishing and maintaining effective working relations with the university community and the public. Ability to maintain composure when faced with difficult situations and to effectively identify and resolve problems and to maintain strict confidentiality related to sensitive information. Demonstrated commitment to diversity and experience promoting inclusive excellence."}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001219944406": {"title": "Athletics Coordinator", "description": "Athletics Coordinator. General Statement of Duties : Plans and executes various activities in support of the Athletics Department. Assists with department finances and budgets, special events, student employment, Human Resources, and day-to-day administrative duties. Serves as the Athletic Department liaison to the Webster University community and outside entities. Exercises discretion and independent judgment in the performance of duties. Reports to the Director of Athletics. Essential Duties and Responsibilities* Performs administrative clerical and office support duties in support of the Director of Athletics and the head coaches. Advises department on university policies and procedures. Assists with budgetary strategies for department entities; monitoring and tracking budget for department and teams, monitoring team specific fundraising initiatives, and reconciling P-Card monthly reports for department staff while ensuring compliance with university policies. Processes all check requisitions and deposits for Athletic Department. Uses financial system to complete and submit annual surveys with U.S. Department of Education and NCAA Gender Equity reports. Provides institutional, departmental, conference and NCAA information in person or by telephone to prospective or current students, parents of current or prospective students, college administrators, alumni, and the public at large. Assists Compliance Officer with NCAA Compliance for the Athletic Department. Assists with maintaining and updating confidential student Compliance records regarding student-athlete eligibility for approximately 280 student-athletes. Assists with records maintenance, organizes and evaluates NCAA paperwork to ensure compliance with University and NCAA policies. Works with head coaches as issues arise with their student-athletes and/or teams and keeps staff up to date with any rule changes. Works with WU Human Resources and Finances to coordinate department\u2019s new hires, promotions, and separation paperwork. Assist with new hire onboarding and address any inquiries about positions during the transition phase. Assists Student Employment Supervisor with student employment positions. Works with Career Services to process paperwork for 85-90 student employees. Assists with paperwork submission, verifying student work study eligibility, and tracking students\u2019 earnings. Responsible for hiring, training and supervising office assistant student workers. Coordinates implementation for department special events including but not limited to the Gorlok Open, senior nights, parents\u2019 receptions, Athletic Awards Ceremony, hosting conference and regional tournaments, reserving rooms for NCAA tournament watch parties, and Hall of Fame Induction. Assists with the negotiation and approval of contacts for team ground travel (buses and rental vans). Confirms game schedules, assist with game day management and operations, including locker room signage, coordinating the reservation of officials parking with Public Safety and reserving rooms in the University Center. Maintains favorable liaison with college campus personnel both internally and externally. Recommends improvements in organization and takes lead on action. Stays abreast of university programs, policies, and procedures. Responds to public inquiries in a courteous manner; receives and resolves student problems and complaints; refers serious problems and complaints to immediate supervisor. Provide operating instruction on various office equipment such as computers, fax, copier, telephone, etc. Performs other duties as assigned. Bachelor's Degree from an accredited college or university. Strong organizational skills, detail-oriented. Ability to work on multiple projects simultaneously. Excellent verbal and written communication skills. Ability to exercise important human relations skills in establishing and maintaining effective working relations with the university community and the public. Ability to maintain composure when faced with difficult situations and to effectively identify and resolve problems and to maintain strict confidentiality related to sensitive information. Demonstrated commitment to diversity and experience promoting inclusive excellence."}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001218417606": {"title": "Centre Francophone (CF) Fellow", "description": "Centre Francophone (CF) Fellow. The CF Fellow serves to promote the Centre Francophone (CF) and French-related events at Webster. This part-time position will be responsible for building and strengthening community relations between the university and French/Francophone related organizations, educational institutions (e.g., high schools, community colleges), and corporate and other partners to attract and generate interest in French study at Webster. Essential Duties and Responsibilities* \u00b7 Facilitate the Seal of Biliteracy recognition by the university \u00b7 Develop internal and external partnerships with French-related organizations (i.e., AATF, Alliance Fran\u00e7aise de St. Louis, et al.), educational institutions, and corporate and other partners to strengthen academic-community partnerships \u00b7 Organize French/Francophone related activities and events at Webster to promote French language and culture to existing and prospective students in collaboration with relevant faculty \u00b7 Conduct outreach to local area educational institutions (high schools, community colleges, et al.) to promote French study at Webster \u00b7 Promote study abroad in French-speaking countries to current students in collaboration with Study Abroad as appropriate \u00b7 Maintain CF website, social media, and other communication platforms as needed Secondary Duties and Responsibilities* \u00b7 Identify opportunities for internships and other experiential learning opportunities for students as appropriate Functional/Technical Skills* \u00b7 Native or fluent French speaker with deep knowledge and understanding of French/Francophone cultures \u00b7 Demonstrated ability to develop partnerships across diverse stakeholders and effectively interact with people from different social and cultural backgrounds \u00b7 Experience organizing educational, cultural, or community events and working collaboratively with student populations \u00b7 Excellent communication skills (written and oral) with timely and professional responsiveness to emails and requests \u00b7 Strong organizational skills and multi-tasker with the ability to handle multiple priorities and consistently meet deadlines \u00b7 Proficiency in Microsoft 365 and ability to learn new software as needed \u00b7 Experience living, studying or traveling in France or French-related countries highly desirable"}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001217229606": {"title": "Sound Design Mentor", "description": "Sound Design Mentor. Webster University\u2019s Sargent Conservatory of Theatre Arts invites applications for an adjunct faculty member \u2014 Sound Design Mentor ( The Crucible ) Essential Duties: Mentoring Sound Design students for Mainstage production in the Fall Meeting with the Sound Design students and attending tech rehearsals for The Crucible Bachelor's degree and/or extensive experience in the field"}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001209610106": {"title": "Adjunct Professor of Living Anatomy and Movement", "description": "Adjunct Professor of Living Anatomy and Movement. Webster University in St. Louis is currently seeking an adjunct instructor for the Dance Program to teach Living Anatomy and Movement, an undergraduate course designed for dance majors. This course explores functional anatomy through movement, helping students develop an understanding of the body's structure and mechanics as they relate to dance technique, performance, conditioning, and injury prevention. Master's degree in Dance, Kinesiology, Exercise Science, Somatics, Physical Therapy, Athletic Training, Anatomy, or a closely related field. (A bachelor's degree with significant professional experience may be considered.)"}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001208854806": {"title": "Adjunct Instructor Communications and Journalism", "description": "Adjunct Instructor Communications and Journalism. Webster University in St. Louis is currently seeking adjunct instructors for the Communications and Journalism Department to teach courses in Journalism, Social Media, Media Analytics, Media Studies, and Strategic Communications which includes Advertising and Public Relations. Applicants must possess a MA degree and a minimum of five years of professional experience in the relevant field. Responsibilities include teaching one or more courses per semester either in classroom or online. Applications will be reviewed on an as-needed basis. Applicants must possess a MA degree and a minimum of five years of professional experience in the relevant field."}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001201272206": {"title": "Adjunct Faculty \u2013 Acting for the Camera", "description": "Adjunct Faculty \u2013 Acting for the Camera. Webster University\u2019s Sargent Conservatory of Theatre Arts invites applications for an adjunct faculty member specializing in Acting for the Camera . Essential Duties - Teach Acting for the Camera \u2013 a unit within CONS 4010 and MUTH 4010 - Attend semester auditions to observe performance majors - Lead weekly classes - Mentor students - Collaborate with performance faculty Bachelor's degree and/or extensive experience in the field"}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001196757006": {"title": "Adjunct Faculty \u2013 Performance Auditions and Casting", "description": "Adjunct Faculty \u2013 Performance Auditions and Casting. Webster University's Sargent Conservatory of Theatre Arts invites applications for an adjunct faculty member, specializing in performance auditions and casting . Essential Duties - Teach 4 th Year Auditions \u2013 a unit within CONS 4010 and MUTH 4010 - Attend semester auditions to observe performance majors - Lead weekly classes - Mentor students - Collaborate with performance faculty Bachelor's degree and/or extensive experience in the field"}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001172817106": {"title": "Accompanist (Music)", "description": "Accompanist (Music). Webster Dance Department is seeking part time accompanists to work with our ballet instructors on faculty for various classes through the week. Experience in classical music and variations for accompanying college level dance major courses."}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001154328206": {"title": "Adjunct Faculty - Business Analytics (Webster San Antonio Metro)", "description": "Adjunct Faculty - Business Analytics (Webster San Antonio Metro). The Department of Computer and Information Science (CAIS) at Webster University in St. Louis, Missouri, is recruiting adjunct faculty to teach the following graduate-level, Business Analytic courses, at the University\u2019s San Antonio Campus in Texas: CSDA 5130 Social and Ethical Issues in Analytics CSBU 5300 Database Systems for Business Analytics CSBU 5320 Data Analytics Foundations for Business Analytics CSBU 5340 ERP Systems and Business Transformation CSDA 5310 Data Visualization CSBU 5420 Machine Learning for Business Analytics CSBU 6300 Descriptive Analytics and ERP Integration Practicum The teaching modalities include: In Person Classroom - On Campus Master\u2019s degree in Business Analytics, Computer Science, Information Systems, Business Administration, or a closely related field from a regionally accredited institution. At least one year of teaching experience at the postsecondary level. Demonstrated experience using or implementing ERP systems, especially SAP S/4HANA. Proficiency in integrated business processes such as Procure-to-Pay, Order-to-Cash, and Plan-to-Produce. Experience in project management and technical leadership responsibilities. Proficiency in data visualization and analysis tools such as Microsoft Excel and Power BI. Excellent interpersonal and communication skills, with a commitment to student success and professional growth. Strong organizational skills and ability to provide timely grading and student support."}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001146758506": {"title": "Criminology & Criminal Justice Adjunct Professor", "description": "Criminology & Criminal Justice Adjunct Professor. The Criminology and Criminal Justice program is seeking an adjunct professor to teach one 16-week section per semester of \u201cCRIM 1050: Introduction to Criminology\u201d or \u201cCRIM 1100: Introduction to Criminal Justice.\u201d CRIM 1050 introduces students to dominant criminological perspectives on why people commit crime, and the patterns and trends of several crime types (violence, property crimes, white collar crime, organized crime, etc). Students will study how cultural, historical, political, and geographical factors affect the patterns of how crime unfolds and the prevalence of crime, especially in the U.S. The course emphasizes criminological theory and the adjunct must be comfortable in this area. CRIM 1100, in contrast, focuses on the functions, development, and efficacy of criminal justice institutions in the U.S. Students will learn about the functions and crime-control capacity of institutions such as the police, criminal courts, corrections, parole, probation, and juvenile justice. The course also addresses the impact of these institutions on different social groups, emphasizing the racial, ethnic, socioeconomic, and gendered impacts of the criminal justice system. Both courses also count towards the Social Systems and Human Behaviors of Webster\u2019s Global Citizenship Program. CRIM 1050 also satisfies the Written Communication requirement, while CRIM 1100 satisfies the Oral Communication requirement; assessment activities for these programs will be required. Both courses are offered on-ground as a lecture course. Class sizes vary from 20 \u2013 30 students. Physical Demands Typical office environment. Location Webster Groves Campus. Minimum Qualifications M.A. or Graduate Certificate in Criminology or related discipline (Sociology, Criminal Justice, Justice Studies, etc.). Relevant teaching and professional experience in the field of criminology and criminal justice. Preferred Qualifications Ph.D. or ABD in fields listed above. Candidates who are also capable of teaching related introductory courses on-ground (such as Introduction to Law, Deviance and Social Control, Introduction to Sociology, or Introduction to Women, Gender, and Sexuality Studies). Department Law, Crime, and Social Justice (which houses the Criminology and Criminal Justice Program) M.A. in Criminology or related field with relevant training and expertise to criminology (Sociology, Criminal Justice, Justice Studies, Legal Studies). Functional/Technical Skills* Basic computer skills (Microsoft Office) PowerPoint or comparable presentation tools"}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001090403006": {"title": "Adjunct Faculty - Applied Behavioral Analysis", "description": "Adjunct Faculty - Applied Behavioral Analysis. The Department of Teacher Education is seeking qualified candidates who are enthusiastic about teaching for ongoing part-time adjunct instructor positions in Applied Behavior Analysis. Teaching assignments will vary based on departmental needs. Candidates are expected to perform the following essential duties: Maintain current knowledge of the discipline. Present course material in a clear, organized manner using methodologies appropriate to the discipline-e.g., lecture, demonstration, technology. Explain course expectations clearly to students. Adhere to the requirements of the department's curriculum. Adhere to all university safety policies regarding classroom equipment and materials. Grade assigned papers, examinations, or projects in a timely manner so that students may benefit from the feedback. Provide students with individualized help and consultation to meet students\u2019 needs. Meet administrative requirements and deadlines such as those for submitting course syllabi, conducting student course evaluations, and submitting final grades. Use technology as established and communicated by the University to carry out the administrative duties associated with teaching. General qualifications should be appropriate for the level of class being taught, preferably a master's degree or higher in Behavior Analysis, Education, Psychology, or a related field and/or several years' professional experience in a relevant field. The applicant MUST hold BCBA certification or higher. The applicant should have experience and/or training relevant to the content area being taught. A willingness to work collaboratively with faculty, as well as teach and mentor students from a wide range of disciplines, cultures, and academic backgrounds is essential."}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001099489806": {"title": "Adjunct Nursing Professor", "description": "Adjunct Nursing Professor. Seeking an experienced nurse educator who is able to teach MSN students in the nurse educator practicum as well as instructional strategies, and other MSN level courses. DNP or PhD in Nursing"}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001098783606": {"title": "Adjunct - Accounting", "description": "Adjunct - Accounting. The Business Department is recruiting adjunct faculty to teach undergraduate accounting courses at the home campus in St. Louis, MO. This may include such courses as audit and intermediate accounting. Daytime availability is preferred. The candidate has one of the two following qualifications: 1. Master of Science in Accountancy or equivalent. 2. A business-related graduate degree, the Certified Public Accountant license or the Certified Management Accountant certification, and at least three years of relevant professional experience."}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001055657006": {"title": "Adjunct Faculty - Math and Statistics (San Antonio Extended Campus)", "description": "Adjunct Faculty - Math and Statistics (San Antonio Extended Campus). Master's degree in statistics, mathematics, applied mathematics, or related field with at least 18 credit hours of graduate coursework in the area of teaching responsibility. Experience in teaching mathematics, statistics to graduate students. Classes are in person on campus and must reside in or near the San Antonio area. Master's degree in statistics, mathematics, applied mathematics, or related field with at least 18 credit hours of graduate coursework in the area of teaching responsibility. Experience in teaching mathematics, statistics to graduate students. Classes are in person on campus and must reside in or near the San Antonio area."}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001050160006": {"title": "Adjunct Instructor \u2013 Audio Aesthetics & Technology", "description": "Adjunct Instructor \u2013 Audio Aesthetics & Technology. The Audio Aesthetics & Technology department in the School of Communications is looking for adjunct instructors - Responsibilities include but are not limited to, teaching 1-3 courses per semester in the subject of, Audio Media with strong communication and interpersonal skills. Commitment to fostering diversity, equity, and inclusion in the classroom. Willingness to engage in ongoing professional development and collaboration with colleagues. Note that this announcement aims to solicit applications in order to identify a pool of adjunct faculty who are qualified to teach undergraduate and/or graduate coursework in various modalities. Teaching assignments vary based on departmental needs. Applicants must possess a BA degree and/or a minimum of five years of professional experience in the relevant industry. Preference will be given to candidates with expertise in any or all of the following: Expertise in any or all of the following areas: sound design, audio recording, editing, mixing, Foley artistry, and field recording. Additional teaching experience and a master\u2019s degree will be a significant advantage."}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001042615706": {"title": "Adjunct Instructor, Information Visualization Course, Interactive Digital Media Program", "description": "Adjunct Instructor, Information Visualization Course, Interactive Digital Media Program. Webster University in St. Louis is currently seeking an adjunct instructor for the Interactive Digital Media Program in the Media Arts Department to teach an information visualization course. This course focuses on graphically communicating complex data and data-driven storytelling. Applicants must possess a BA degree and/or a minimum of five years of professional experience in the relevant industry. Preference will be given to candidates with expertise in graphic design and motion graphics. Additional teaching experience and a master\u2019s degree will be significant advantages. Note that this announcement aims to solicit applications in order to identify a pool of adjunct faculty who are qualified to teach undergraduate and/or graduate coursework in various modalities. Teaching assignments vary based on departmental needs. Applicants must possess a BA degree and/or a minimum of five years of professional experience in the relevant industry. Preference will be given to candidates with expertise in graphic design and motion graphics. Additional teaching experience and a master\u2019s degree will be significant advantages."}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001041545906": {"title": "Adjunct - Community Music School", "description": "Adjunct - Community Music School. Instruct students of various ages and skill levels (beginning, intermediate, and/or advanced) on instrument via online or in-person individual 30, 45, or 60-minute lessons; Correspond with parents/guardians; Attend CMS faculty meetings; Encourage students to participate in CMS recitals; May assist with preparation for competitions and/or festivals Teaching observation/video may be required Bachelors; Prior private lesson teaching required"}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001015827706": {"title": "Adjunct Instructor - Film, Television & Video Production", "description": "Adjunct Instructor - Film, Television & Video Production. Webster University in St. Louis is currently seeking an adjunct instructor for the Film, Television, and Video Production Program in the Media Arts Department to teach beginning to intermediate-level film production courses. Applicants must possess a BA degree and/or a minimum of five years of professional experience in the relevant industry. Preference will be given to candidates with expertise in any or all of the following: Film and TV Producing, Directing, Cinematography, Editing, Production Design, or Grip and Electric. Additional teaching experience and a master\u2019s degree will be significant advantages. Note that this announcement aims to solicit applications in order to identify a pool of adjunct faculty who are qualified to teach undergraduate and/or graduate coursework in various modalities. Teaching assignments vary based on departmental needs. Applicants must possess a BA degree and/or a minimum of five years of professional experience in the relevant industry of Film, Television and Video Production. Additional teaching experience and a master\u2019s degree will be significant advantages."}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5000967131906": {"title": "Adjunct Instructor Environmental Management", "description": "Adjunct Instructor Environmental Management. Adjunct instructor position for the Masters in Environmental Management program. Teaching will be in person at the Webster Groves, MO campus, in evenings. Masters in Environmental Science, Environmental Management, Environmental Regulations or related field. Knowledge of current statutory, regulatory approaches to environmental management and operations. Understand project management processes and analytical tools to achieve a sustainable outcome to environmental problems."}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5000989273106": {"title": "Adjunct Instructor for Mathematics and Statistics", "description": "Adjunct Instructor for Mathematics and Statistics. The Department of Natural Sciences and Mathematics is seeking adjunct instructors for MATH and STAT courses. These courses may be in person at our Webster Groves campus or online instruction, depending on the scheduling needs of the department. We are particularly interested in individuals with prior teaching experience in Math or Statistics to undergraduate students. Master\u2019s degree in statistics, mathematics, applied mathematics, or related field with at least 18 credit hours of graduate coursework in the area of teaching responsibility. \u201cEquivalent tested experience\u201d (outside of the classroom) can be used in place of the 18 graduate credit hours of coursework."}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5000963411800": {"title": "Adjunct Instructor for Biological Sciences (Biology, Chemistry, Physics, Exercise Science)", "description": "Adjunct Instructor for Biological Sciences (Biology, Chemistry, Physics, Exercise Science). This is a CONTINUOUS OPEN ANNOUNCEMENT for the Webster University Biological Sciences Adjunct Instructor Pool. Webster University seeks to maintain a pool of individuals who are qualified and willing to provide instruction for Biology, Chemistry, Physics and Exercise Sciences. Individuals with experience and appropriate credentials in these areas are encouraged to apply. Successful candidates will be required to comply with internal academic policies and with external accreditation agencies standards/requirements in order to retain appointment. Essential Job Duties and Responsibilities Adjuncts are hired on a part-time basis to augment the instructional needs that current faculty members are not available to meet. Qualified Instructors will be chosen from the Adjunct Instructor Pool to teach as specific needs arise. Courses may be online or in person. Modality may be in person lecture discussions or laboratory. Qualifications A Master's degree within the discipline (Biology, Chemistry, Physics, ) with a terminal degree highly desired Excellent communication skills Availability for on campus instruction for traditional course delivery Qualifications ( Desired ) Significant applied experience within the professional setting of identified need Prior teaching experience Publication and/or professional experience Experience with online instruction Master's degree within appropriate scientific field (Biology, Chemistry, Physics) or related field from an accredited institution of higher learning. Professional experience in science or a related field. This position is specifically to teach in St Louis, MO"}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5000959538000": {"title": "Adjunct - Biological Sciences", "description": "Adjunct - Biological Sciences. Webster University seeks adjunct faculty. Qualifications require terminal degree (Ph.D. or equivalent master's level in subject area or closely related field). Relevant teaching and professional experience which provides an interdisciplinary base of knowledge."}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5000961204900": {"title": "Adjunct Faculty - San Antonio", "description": "Adjunct Faculty - San Antonio. Webster University seeks adjunct faculty at extended campuses across the United States. Qualifications require terminal degree (Ph.D. or equivalent master's level in subject area or closely related field). Relevant teaching and professional experience which provides an interdisciplinary base of knowledge."}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5000956302700": {"title": "Adjunct Faculty in Music", "description": "Adjunct Faculty in Music. The instructor will be responsible to deliver required meetings per semester, monitor progress of students toward the completion of the instruction and final exam or jury at the end of the semester. Supervision will be under the auspices of the faculty director of the area of study and the Chair of the Department of Music. Essential Duties and Responsibilities: - Develop syllabi appropriate to each course and level of degree instruction. - Provide all course instruction/lessons on the campus of Webster University. - Evaluate and develop weekly progress of students appropriate to course study.- Position is <29 contact hours/week. Part Time. Master of Arts in Music, Master of Music degree, or advanced degree such as D.M.A. or Ph.D. Demonstrated ability as a performer and teacher of Music Minimum three years of successful experience teaching at the University/Professional level"}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5000875599406": {"title": "Adjunct Faculty - Department of Teacher Education", "description": "Adjunct Faculty - Department of Teacher Education. Adjunct Faculty - Department of Teacher Education - Applicant Pool Applications will be held in an applicant pool that will be reviewed when a position opens in a given discipline, to fill that position. Applicants will not be notified of their status. Selected applicants will be contacted for an interview after an opening necessitates consulting the pool. Applicants are encouraged to indicate their availability by completing the Adjunct Questionaire. The Department of Teacher Education is seeking qualified candidates who are enthusiastic about teaching for ongoing part-time adjunct instructor positions. Teaching assignments will vary based on departmental needs. Candidates are expected to perform the following essential duties: Maintain current knowledge of the discipline. Present course material in a clear, organized manner using methodologies appropriate to the discipline-e.g., lecture, demonstration, technology. Explain course expectations clearly to students. Adhere to the requirements of the department's curriculum. Adhere to all university safety policies regarding classroom equipment and materials. Grade assigned papers, examinations, or projects in a timely manner so that students may benefit from the feedback. Provide students with individualized help and consultation to meet students\u2019 needs. Meet administrative requirements and deadlines such as those for submitting course syllabi, conducting student course evaluations, and submitting final grades. Use technology as established and communicated by the University to carry out the administrative duties associated with teaching. General qualifications should be appropriate for the level of class being taught, preferably a master's degree or higher in Education or a related field and/or several years' professional experience in a relevant field . The applicant should have experience and/or training relevant to the content area being taught. A willingness to work collaboratively with faculty, as well as teach and mentor students from a wide range of disciplines, cultures, and academic backgrounds is essential."}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5000871397006": {"title": "Adjunct Faculty Pool - Psychology", "description": "Adjunct Faculty Pool - Psychology. The Department of Psychology is seeking qualified candidates who are enthusiastic about teaching for ongoing part-time adjunct instructor positions. Note that this announcement aims to solicit applications in order to identify a pool of adjunct faculty who are qualified to teach undergraduate and/or graduate coursework in various modalities. Teaching assignments vary based on departmental needs. General qualifications should be appropriate for the level of class being taught, typically a Ph.D. in Psychology or related field. An ABD currently enrolled graduate student will be considered in special cases. The applicant should have experience and/or training relevant to the content area(a) being taught. Willingness to work collaboratively with faculty, as well as teach and mentor students from a wide range of disciplines, cultures, and academic backgrounds is essential."}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5000868289106": {"title": "Game Design Production, Adjunct Faculty", "description": "Game Design Production, Adjunct Faculty. Webster University, School of Communications is seeking an adjunct faculty member to teach in the Media Arts Department / Game Design Production course. Experience and teaching success in the area of Game & Game Design. Webster University, School of Communications is seeking an adjunct faculty member to teach in the Media Arts Department / Game Design Production course. Experience and teaching success in the area of Game & Game Design required."}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5000857329206": {"title": "Adjunct Faculty - Management", "description": "Adjunct Faculty - Management. The Department of Management at Webster University in St. Louis, Missouri, is recruiting adjunct faculty to teach in the Management Department programs. Graduate level courses typically meet one evening per week for nine weeks. Undergrad courses either meet once a week for 8 weeks or during the day (number of days and times vary per course) over 16 weeks. Faculty teaching at the graduate level shall have at least one of the following: A minimum of a terminal degree (as defined by the discipline) in the same or closely related discipline as the course(s) taught. Alternatively, a minimum of a terminal degree (as defined by the discipline) with eighteen (18) or more earned credit hours in the same or closely related discipline as the courses taught. Evidence of tested experience appropriate to the specific teaching assignment."}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001233965600": {"title": "Sr. Human Resources Business Partner - Academic Affairs", "description": "Sr. Human Resources Business Partner - Academic Affairs. The Senior Human Resources Business Partner (Senior HRBP) \u2013 Academic Affairs serves as the first point of contact for faculty human resources matters and maintains a direct, embedded working partnership with Academic Affairs. The role provides day-to-day strategic and operational HR consultation to Deans, Department Chairs, and other academic leaders across the Schools and Colleges. Reporting directly to the Associate Vice President, People & Culture, the Senior HRBP translates institutional people priorities into consistent faculty employment practices. The AVP retains primary HR partnership responsibility with the University\u2019s executive leadership team, including executive-level strategic and organizational matters. The Senior HRBP is a key partner in faculty recruitment and selection, including full-time and adjunct faculty, working closely with Academic Affairs hiring leaders and Talent Acquisition. The role maintains a dotted-line, functional relationship with Academic Affairs Administrative Managers to coordinate faculty employment workflows, standardize processes, and resolve operational issues. Administrative Managers retain their formal reporting relationships and personnel supervision within Academic Affairs. Essential Duties and Responsibilities 1. Direct Academic Affairs Partnership & Faculty First-Contact Support (30%) Serve as the first point of contact for faculty HR questions, concerns, and employment matters; provide responsive consultation and coordinate resolution with Benefits, Payroll, Talent Acquisition, HR Operations, Employee Relations, and Legal as appropriate. Serve as the direct HR partner and trusted advisor to Deans, Department Chairs, and academic leaders on faculty workforce planning, employment decisions, performance, organizational changes, and people-management practices. Elevate executive-level matters to the AVP, who serves as the primary HR partner to the executive leadership team. Provide timely, confidential guidance on faculty employment policies, escalation pathways, and institutional practices; identify emerging workforce risks and recommend solutions. 2. Faculty Recruitment, Selection & Employment Administration (30%) Serve as a key HR partner in recruitment and selection of full-time and adjunct faculty, advising Deans, Department Chairs, and search committees on recruitment planning, selection processes, equitable and consistent practices, hiring approvals, and offer coordination in partnership with Talent Acquisition and Academic Affairs. Coordinate faculty employment lifecycle processes, including appointments, contracts, adjunct assignments, onboarding, promotions, tenure-related employment actions, compensation changes, leaves, retirements, and separations. Maintain a dotted-line functional partnership with Academic Affairs Administrative Managers to coordinate full-time and adjunct faculty hiring workflows, appointments, contracts, documentation, and timely HR transactions; provide process guidance and escalation support without assuming direct personnel supervision. Coordinate faculty employment records, Faculty Master Summary reporting, data integrity, and workforce reporting with designated institutional owners. Partner with the appropriate offices on student employment matters affecting Academic Affairs and on immigration sponsorship and work authorization. 3. Talent Partnership & Organizational Effectiveness (15%) Partner with Talent Management & Organizational Effectiveness to implement performance management, leadership development, engagement, succession planning, and talent review initiatives across Academic Affairs. Advise academic leaders on workforce trends, leadership capability, retention, and organizational effectiveness using relevant workforce data and measures. 4. HR Operations, Compliance & Governance (15%) Advise on consistent application of employment policies and refer specialized matters involving ADA, FMLA, wage and hour, Title IX, immigration, and other legal requirements to accountable subject-matter owners. Support audit readiness, process documentation, records accuracy, faculty employment controls, and timely escalation of complex or sensitive issues. Coordinate process improvements with central HR, Academic Affairs, Payroll, Finance, and Legal to reduce errors and strengthen service delivery. 5. Cross-Functional Coordination & Service Excellence (10%) Maintain a direct, ongoing service relationship with Academic Affairs leaders and a dotted-line functional relationship with Academic Affairs Administrative Managers, establishing service expectations, escalation protocols, and shared guidance for faculty recruitment and employment processes. Provide HR policy and process education to academic leaders and Administrative Managers; support cross-training and business continuity. Represent HR on relevant Academic Affairs committees and university-wide initiatives; perform related duties as assigned. Salary for this position: $80,000-$90,000 annually Functional / Technical Skills Exceptional communication, consultation, negotiation, and relationship-building skills; ability to establish trusted partnerships with Deans, Department Chairs, Administrative Managers, and other academic leaders. Demonstrated expertise in HR business partnership, faculty and staff employment administration, employee relations, workforce planning, compensation, performance management, and employment law. Strong knowledge of higher education employment practices, including full-time and adjunct faculty recruitment and selection, appointments, contracts, promotions, tenure, leaves, compensation actions, and academic employment processes. Ability to advise Deans, Department Chairs, and academic leaders on complex workforce matters, applying University policies consistently and escalating executive-level matters through the AVP, People & Culture. Strong project management, operational coordination, coaching, conflict resolution, and change-management skills; ability to influence outcomes and guide Administrative Managers through a dotted-line functional relationship without direct supervisory authority. Ability to analyze workforce data, identify employment and operational risks, and recommend practical, institutionally aligned solutions. Experience coordinating cross-functional initiatives with Academic Affairs, Talent Acquisition, Payroll, Benefits, Finance, Legal, and other HR partners. Working knowledge of ADA, FMLA, wage and hour requirements, immigration sponsorship, and applicable federal and state employment laws; sound judgment regarding referrals to designated compliance specialists. Proficiency with HRIS, workforce analytics, Microsoft Office, and enterprise systems; commitment to service excellence, confidentiality, process improvement, and institutional stewardship. Requisite Experience and Education Bachelor\u2019s degree in Human Resources, Business Administration, Higher Education Administration, Organizational Leadership, or a related field required; master\u2019s degree preferred. Seven (7) to ten (10) years of progressively responsible HR experience, including HR business partnership, senior-level consultation, or equivalent leadership responsibilities. Experience supporting academic or other senior organizational leaders in a complex, matrixed organization; higher education and faculty employment experience strongly preferred. Demonstrated experience coordinating professional colleagues and cross-functional teams, leading HR projects, and implementing workforce planning, employment governance, and organizational change initiatives. Direct supervisory experience is beneficial but not required."}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001233343306": {"title": "Sr. Director, Office of Global Education and Academic Innovation", "description": "Sr. Director, Office of Global Education and Academic Innovation. Reporting to the Vice Provost of Academic Affairs, the Senior Director provides strategic and operational leadership across functions that advance Webster University\u2019s global academic mission, instructional quality, faculty development, and academic innovation. The Senior Director provides leadership and coordination across Global Education and Planning, Study Abroad and WINS, the Online Learning Center, and the Faculty Development Center. The position translates institutional priorities into operational strategies, strengthens integration across functions, and ensures effective execution, resource stewardship, talent leadership, and organizational performance across Webster\u2019s global network and instructional modalities. ESSENTIAL DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES* Provide strategic and operational leadership across Global Education and Planning, Study Abroad and WINS, the Online Learning Center, and the Faculty Development Center. Partner with the Vice Provost to translate institutional priorities into strategies, operating plans, and measurable outcomes. Lead initiatives that advance global education, student mobility, online learning, faculty development, instructional quality, and academic innovation across locations and modalities. Drive organizational effectiveness and continuous improvement by strengthening processes, accountability, service delivery, and implementation of change. Use data and performance measures to evaluate outcomes, identify opportunities and risks, and inform decision-making. Lead, develop, and manage assigned staff, establishing clear expectations and accountability for performance. Manage assigned budgets and resources to ensure effective financial stewardship and alignment with institutional priorities. Build effective partnerships with faculty, deans, academic leaders, global campuses, and administrative functions to advance shared priorities. Ensure appropriate policies, procedures, controls, and operating standards are maintained and represent the Office on institutional initiatives and external partnerships as assigned. Perform other duties as assigned. SECONDARY DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES* Prepare reports, presentations, analyses, and recommendations for academic and institutional leadership. Support accreditation, assessment, compliance, and institutional reporting activities related to assigned areas. Salary range for this position: $95,000-$110,000 annually. Any one position may not include all duties listed, nor do the examples necessarily include all duties performed. Education: Master\u2019s degree in higher education administration, education, international education, organizational leadership, instructional design, or related field required. Functional/Technical Skills and Leadership Competencies* Strategic Execution: Translates institutional priorities into actionable plans and measurable results. Financial Management: Effectively manages budgets, resources, and competing priorities. Talent Leadership: Builds effective teams, develops talent, manages performance, and creates accountability. Organizational Effectiveness: Aligns people, processes, and resources to strengthen organizational performance. Decision Making: Uses data, sound judgment, and institutional context to make effective decisions. Change Leadership: Leads organizational change and supports successful implementation and adoption. Relationship Management: Builds effective partnerships and influences across academic and administrative functions. Demonstrated knowledge of higher education academic operations and administration. Strong strategic planning, analytical, project management, and process-improvement capabilities. Ability to lead complex, cross-functional initiatives in a matrixed organization. Strong written, verbal, facilitation, and presentation skills. REQUISITE EXPERIENCE* 8\u201312+ years of progressively responsible experience with significant leadership experience in higher education, academic administration, global education, online learning, faculty development, instructional innovation, or a related field. Demonstrated experience leading multiple functions, complex programs, or significant organizational initiatives. Experience leading and developing managers and professional staff. Demonstrated budget and resource management experience. Experience leading strategic execution, organizational change, and process improvement. Demonstrated ability to partner effectively with senior leaders, faculty, academic leadership, and cross-functional stakeholders. Experience within a multi-campus, global, international, or distributed academic environment strongly preferred."}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001233342906": {"title": "Director, Academic Administration & Planning", "description": "Director, Academic Administration & Planning. The Director, Academic Administration & Planning provides operational, financial, planning, analytical, and administrative leadership for Academic Affairs and reports directly to the Provost. The position coordinates the infrastructure, analysis, resource planning, and administrative processes necessary to support the Provost, academic leadership, schools, colleges, and other Academic Affairs units across the University. The Director translates academic and institutional priorities into coordinated operational and financial plans, strengthens resource stewardship, and provides analysis and recommendations to support effective executive decision-making. The position serves as a key administrative partner to the Provost and works across Academic Affairs to strengthen operational effectiveness, financial discipline, planning, accountability, and continuity. The Director partners with Human Resources, as appropriate, on matters affecting Academic Affairs. The role requires strong operational leadership, financial acumen, strategic and resource planning, data-informed decision-making, project and process management, cross-functional collaboration, organizational effectiveness, and the ability to translate complex information into actionable recommendations. Essential Duties & Responsibilities Academic Affairs Budget & Financial Management \u2014 35% Lead and coordinate the development, administration, monitoring, forecasting, and analysis of the Academic Affairs budget in partnership with the Provost, Finance, Deans, and academic leadership. Develop budget forecasts, financial analyses, and resource-allocation scenarios to support academic priorities, financial sustainability, and executive decision-making. Monitor expenditures, commitments, budget variances, resource utilization, and emerging financial risks and escalate significant concerns to the Provost and appropriate institutional partners. Coordinate annual budget planning and recurring financial reporting for Academic Affairs. Develop and maintain appropriate financial documentation, controls, reconciliation practices, and reporting mechanisms. Advise the Provost on budget performance, resource utilization, financial risks, and opportunities for improved efficiency and resource alignment. Serve as a primary Academic Affairs liaison with Finance on budget, financial planning, and resource matters. Academic Administration & Resource Planning \u2014 25% Lead and coordinate administrative and resource planning across Academic Affairs in alignment with academic and institutional priorities. Assess resource utilization, operational capacity, administrative practices, and opportunities for improved effectiveness and efficiency. Develop and recommend resource-planning and allocation scenarios to support schools, colleges, and Academic Affairs units. Develop and maintain standardized administrative workflows, procedures, documentation, controls, and operating practices. Maintain business continuity documentation and support continuity planning for critical Academic Affairs administrative functions. Identify and escalate operational risks, gaps, duplication, resource constraints, and opportunities for process improvement. Partner with academic and administrative leaders to strengthen coordination and effective use of institutional resources. Academic Planning & Administrative Coordination \u2014 15% Coordinate planning and implementation activities supporting Academic Affairs priorities and initiatives. Translate academic priorities into implementation plans, timelines, milestones, resource requirements, and accountability mechanisms. Coordinate activities across schools, colleges, and Academic Affairs units when centralized administrative support or cross-functional coordination is required. Monitor implementation progress, identify barriers and dependencies, and provide status reporting and recommendations to the Provost. Lead or support continuous improvement of Academic Affairs administrative practices and operating processes. Support implementation of organizational, administrative, and process changes approved by the Provost. Academic Affairs Data, Reporting & Analysis \u2014 15% Develop operational and financial reports, dashboards, forecasts, analyses, and executive decision-support materials for the Provost and academic leadership. Analyze budget, resource, operational, and other relevant Academic Affairs data to identify trends, risks, opportunities, and potential interventions. Establish recurring reporting and decision-support mechanisms that strengthen transparency and accountability. Partner with Finance, Institutional Research, Information Technology, and other institutional data owners to promote accuracy, consistency, and appropriate use of information. Translate complex operational and financial information into clear findings, recommendations, and decision points for academic leadership. Support data-informed assessment of resource allocation, administrative effectiveness, and institutional priorities. Provost & Academic Leadership Support \u2014 10% Provide operational, administrative, planning, financial, and analytical support to the Provost. Prepare analyses, presentations, recommendations, scenarios, and executive decision-support materials. Coordinate assigned operational and administrative initiatives on behalf of the Office of the Provost. Build effective working relationships with Deans, academic leaders, Finance, Institutional Research, Human Resources, Information Technology, and other institutional partners. Represent Academic Affairs on operational committees, planning groups, and institutional initiatives as assigned. Provide recommendations to the Provost regarding administrative effectiveness, resource utilization, operational risks, and opportunities for improvement. The salary range for this position is: $80,000-$90,000 annually. Education Bachelor\u2019s degree in higher education administration, business administration, finance, public administration, organizational leadership, or a related field. Requisite Experience 7\u201310 years of progressively responsible experience in higher education administration, finance, operations, planning, or a related area, including management or significant functional leadership responsibility. Demonstrated experience with budget management, financial planning, forecasting, financial analysis, and resource allocation. Demonstrated experience supporting senior or executive leadership and translating strategic priorities into operational and administrative plans. Demonstrated experience leading complex projects, process improvements, and cross-functional initiatives. Demonstrated experience assessing operational processes, identifying gaps and risks, and developing sustainable solutions. Experience with data analysis, reporting, dashboards, forecasting, and executive decision support. Demonstrated ability to develop recommendations based on financial, operational, and organizational analysis. Demonstrated ability to collaborate effectively with academic and administrative leadership and work across organizational boundaries. Strong project management, organizational, analytical, communication, and problem-solving capabilities. Supervisory experience preferred. Experience within a multi-campus, global, online, or similarly complex higher-education environment preferred. Core Competencies Academic Administration & Planning Operational Leadership & Execution Financial Acumen & Resource Stewardship Strategic & Resource Planning Data-Informed Decision-Making Business & Operational Analysis Project & Process Management Continuous Improvement Executive Decision Support Cross-Functional Collaboration Communication & Influence Relationship Management Risk Identification & Escalation Accountability & Results Orientation Expected Leadership Behaviors Accountable | Collaborative | Analytical | Proactive | Solutions-Oriented | Operationally Disciplined | Data-Informed | Institutionally Focused"}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001233342406": {"title": "Vice Provost, Academic Affairs", "description": "Vice Provost, Academic Affairs. The Vice Provost serves as a senior academic executive with institution-wide responsibility for academic programs, student pathways and success, academic administration, enrollment partnership, and global academic innovation. Reporting directly to the Provost, the Vice Provost leads complex academic portfolios and institution-wide initiatives, establishes priorities and accountability, and exercises delegated authority on behalf of the Provost, including serving as the Provost\u2019s primary designee when unavailable. As a senior advisor to the Provost, the Vice Provost partners with Deans, faculty, shared governance, Strategic Enrollment Management, and institutional leaders to advance academic strategy, new-student recruitment and enrollment growth, student success, institutional effectiveness, and innovation. The Vice Provost serves as the primary Academic Affairs executive liaison to Strategic Enrollment Management, ensuring strong alignment among academic programs, enrollment priorities, market demand, student pathways, and institutional capacity. The role requires executive-level strategic thinking, academic and organizational leadership, change leadership, data-informed decision-making, agility, executive communication, cross-functional influence, and sound executive judgment within Webster\u2019s global, multi-campus environment. Areas of Executive Responsibility Academic Programs and General Education Registrar and Academic Records Transfer Pathways and Articulation Commencement Student Success, Academic Advising, and Academic Support Academic Affairs Partnership with Strategic Enrollment Management Global Education and Planning Study Abroad and WINS Online Learning Faculty Development and Academic Innovation Essential Duties & Responsibilities Academic Programs, Pathways & Student Success \u2014 35% Provide executive leadership for academic programs, general education, academic pathways, advising, academic support, retention, and degree completion. Align academic offerings and pathways with student needs, new-student recruitment opportunities, enrollment demand, market conditions, academic quality, and institutional priorities. Serve as the senior Academic Affairs liaison and strategic partner to Strategic Enrollment Management, strengthening coordination among Enrollment Management, Deans, academic programs, faculty, and other academic stakeholders. Partner with Strategic Enrollment Management to support new-student recruitment, conversion, and enrollment growth by ensuring appropriate academic engagement in recruitment initiatives, prospective-student experiences, program promotion, and enrollment priorities. Use enrollment trends, market demand, student-interest data, and academic performance information in partnership with Strategic Enrollment Management to identify opportunities for program growth, new student pipelines, transfer pathways, and academic portfolio alignment. Engage Deans and other academic leaders in establishing appropriate shared accountability for enrollment outcomes, including academic participation in recruitment, conversion, and yield strategies. Advance transfer, articulation, academic policy, and student-success strategies that improve access, progression, retention, and degree completion. Establish measurable outcomes and accountability across assigned areas. Academic Administration & Institutional Effectiveness \u2014 25% Provide executive oversight of the Registrar, academic records, registration, graduation, commencement, and related academic administrative functions. Ensure consistent application of academic policies and effective practices across schools, colleges, locations, and instructional modalities. Use academic, enrollment, student-success, operational, and financial data to strengthen institutional effectiveness, resource allocation, and decision-making. Assess the effectiveness of assigned academic and administrative functions and recommend changes to organizational structures, processes, resources, technology, and practices when necessary. Advance operational effectiveness, accountability, and continuous improvement across assigned portfolios. Global Education, Faculty Development & Academic Innovation \u2014 20% Provide strategic leadership for global education, study abroad, online learning, faculty development, and academic innovation. Strengthen academic quality and consistency across Webster\u2019s global, multi-campus, and multimodal environment. Advance innovative approaches to teaching, learning, faculty development, academic delivery, and student engagement. Identify opportunities to leverage online, global, and innovative academic offerings to support student access, enrollment opportunities, and institutional growth. Partner with academic leaders to ensure faculty development and academic innovation support institutional priorities, student needs, and evolving educational and market expectations. Executive Leadership & Institutional Strategy \u2014 20% Serve as senior advisor to the Provost and as the Provost\u2019s primary designee when assigned or unavailable. Serve as the primary Academic Affairs executive liaison to Strategic Enrollment Management, ensuring sustained coordination between academic and enrollment strategies, priorities, initiatives, and performance. Partner with Enrollment Management and academic leadership to identify and address barriers to recruitment and enrollment growth and ensure academic programs, faculty engagement, resources, and student pathways appropriately support institutional enrollment objectives. Lead complex, cross-functional academic initiatives and translate institutional priorities into measurable outcomes. Assess institutional and academic conditions, identify emerging opportunities and risks, and demonstrate the agility to adjust priorities, strategies, and resources as conditions change. Establish accountability and performance expectations across assigned portfolios. Develop senior leaders and strengthen organizational capacity, succession readiness, and operational continuity. Build effective partnerships with Deans, faculty, shared governance, executive leadership, and institutional partners. Provide the Provost with strategic analysis, recommendations, and executive-level insight regarding academic performance, student success, enrollment alignment, organizational effectiveness, and institutional priorities. The salary range for this position is: $180,000-$195,000 annually. Education & Experience Master\u2019s degree required 12\u201315+ years of progressively responsible higher-education leadership experience, including substantial senior-level leadership. Demonstrated experience leading multiple academic functions, complex portfolios, and institution-wide initiatives. Demonstrated success partnering with Deans, faculty, shared governance, and senior institutional leaders. Demonstrated experience partnering with Enrollment Management, Admissions, or related functions to support student recruitment, enrollment growth, academic program demand, and student success. Experience across academic programs, student success, academic administration, and organizational change. Demonstrated experience assessing organizational or academic operations and translating findings into strategic and operational improvements. Demonstrated experience using academic, enrollment, operational, market, and financial data to support institutional decision-making and resource allocation. Demonstrated ability to lead effectively in a changing higher-education environment, including the ability to reassess priorities, address barriers, and adjust strategies when institutional or market conditions warrant. Experience in a global, multi-campus, online, or similarly complex higher-education environment preferred. Strategic Leadership Competencies Executive & Academic Leadership \u2014 Leads complex portfolios, establishes accountability, develops leaders, and exercises sound executive judgment. Strategy, Enrollment Partnership & Institutional Effectiveness \u2014 Translates institutional priorities into measurable academic and student-success outcomes and builds strong partnerships between Academic Affairs and Strategic Enrollment Management to advance recruitment, enrollment, retention, and institutional sustainability. Academic Governance & Partnership \u2014 Effectively engages Deans, faculty, shared governance, Enrollment Management, and institutional partners while navigating complex academic and institutional environments. Organizational & Change Leadership \u2014 Assesses organizational effectiveness, leads change, develops leaders, aligns resources, and strengthens institutional capacity and continuity. Financial & Data-Informed Decision-Making \u2014 Uses academic, enrollment, student-success, operational, market, and financial data to inform strategy, resource allocation, and executive decision-making. Agility & Strategic Judgment \u2014 Anticipates emerging challenges and opportunities, reassesses assumptions, and adjusts strategies and priorities as institutional needs and external conditions evolve. Executive Communication & Influence \u2014 Communicates effectively at the executive level, builds alignment across organizational boundaries, and influences outcomes through collaboration, credibility, and sound judgment."}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001233341906": {"title": "Assistant Vice Provost, Academic Quality & Accreditation", "description": "Assistant Vice Provost, Academic Quality & Accreditation. The Assistant Vice Provost, Academic Quality & Accreditation provides university-wide strategic and operational leadership for academic quality, institutional and specialized accreditation, academic compliance, policy governance, and continuous improvement across Webster University\u2019s schools, colleges, academic programs, instructional locations, and modalities. Reporting to the Provost, the role leads multiple functional areas and institution-wide initiatives, exercises cross-functional decision authority, and translates institutional strategy, accreditation standards, and regulatory requirements into effective operational practices. The Assistant Vice Provost serves as a senior advisor to the Provost and partners with Deans, faculty, shared governance, and administrative leaders to strengthen academic quality, institutional accountability, compliance, and continuous improvement. The role requires executive-level strategic thinking, institutional effectiveness, policy development, change leadership, executive communication, data-informed decision-making, organizational leadership, relationship management, and cross-functional influence, along with the judgment and executive presence necessary to navigate complex and sensitive academic matters. This scope reflects the complexity, institutional impact, decision authority, and accountability expected of Webster\u2019s Assistant Vice Provost level. Essential Duties & Responsibilities Institutional & Specialized Accreditation \u2013 30% Provide university-wide leadership for Higher Learning Commission (HLC) accreditation, reaffirmation, reporting, monitoring, and institutional readiness. Coordinate accreditation reviews, evidence collection, institutional reports, site visits, and required follow-up. Maintain an institution-wide accreditation calendar, documentation framework, and readiness process. Coordinate and monitor specialized and programmatic accreditation requirements across schools, colleges, and academic programs. Advise the Provost, Deans, faculty, and academic leaders regarding accreditation standards, risks, findings, and required actions. Serve as the University\u2019s HLC Accreditation Liaison Officer, if formally designated. Academic Compliance & State Authorization \u2013 20% Provide leadership for assigned federal and state academic compliance requirements. Oversee and coordinate state authorization requirements affecting academic programs, instructional locations, online education, and other academic activities. Monitor regulatory changes and translate requirements into appropriate institutional practices. Establish processes to identify, document, escalate, and remediate academic compliance risks. Coordinate with Legal, Registrar, Institutional Research, Financial Aid, Finance, and other institutional partners as appropriate. Academic Policy, Governance & Quality \u2013 20% Lead the development, review, coordination, and implementation of University-level academic policies. Establish and maintain an effective academic policy review and governance process. Partner with faculty, Deans, academic leadership, and shared governance to promote consistent and appropriate implementation of academic policies. Coordinate assigned student academic complaint processes and identify systemic trends requiring institutional attention. Promote consistent academic quality and continuous improvement practices across locations and modalities. Academic Oversight, Reporting & Continuous Improvement \u2013 20% Establish mechanisms for monitoring academic quality, compliance, accreditation readiness, and corrective actions. Develop dashboards, reports, compliance calendars, and risk indicators for the Provost and academic leadership. Coordinate grade submission oversight and partner with academic leaders and the Registrar to address recurring issues. Analyze academic quality, accreditation, policy, complaint, and compliance trends and recommend improvements. Serve as the Academic Affairs liaison for assigned grant and Institutional Review Board (IRB) matters. Maintain appropriate documentation, workflows, controls, and business continuity practices for critical functions. Institutional Leadership & Partnership \u2013 10% Serve as a senior advisor to the Provost regarding academic quality, accreditation, compliance, and institutional risk. Lead cross-functional initiatives involving multiple schools, colleges, academic units, and administrative functions. Build effective partnerships with faculty, Deans, shared governance, Registrar, Institutional Research, Legal, Student Affairs, and other University stakeholders. Represent Academic Affairs on institutional committees and external matters within assigned areas of responsibility. Lead and develop assigned professional and administrative staff. Salary range for this position: $138,000-$142,000 annually. Education Master\u2019s degree in higher education administration, education, academic affairs, institutional effectiveness, public administration, or a related field. Requisite Experience 8\u201312+ years of progressively responsible management experience, including significant higher education leadership experience, consistent with Webster\u2019s Assistant Vice Provost framework. Demonstrated experience with institutional accreditation, preferably Higher Learning Commission accreditation. Experience in academic quality, institutional effectiveness, academic compliance, assessment, academic policy, or related functions. Demonstrated experience leading complex cross-functional or institution-wide initiatives. Demonstrated experience working collaboratively with faculty, Deans, academic leaders, and shared governance. Experience interpreting accreditation standards, regulatory requirements, and institutional policies. Experience developing institutional reports, accreditation materials, compliance documentation, and executive recommendations. Supervisory or significant team leadership experience. Experience within a multi-campus, global, online, or similarly complex higher education environment preferred. Core Leadership Competencies Executive & Strategic Leadership Accreditation & Academic Quality Academic Policy & Governance Regulatory Compliance & Risk Management Institutional Effectiveness Operational Leadership & Execution Data-Informed Decision-Making Organizational & Change Leadership Continuous Improvement Executive Communication & Influence Cross-Functional Collaboration Relationship Management Faculty & Shared Governance Partnership Resource Management Expected Leadership Behaviors: Accountable | Collaborative | Proactive | Solutions-Oriented | Operationally Disciplined | Data-Informed | Institutionally Focused | Decisive | Consultative"}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001232541606": {"title": "Professor of Practice - Nurse Anesthesia Program", "description": "Professor of Practice - Nurse Anesthesia Program. This position will begin during the 2026-2027 academic year, starting in November 2026. The Department of Advanced Clinical Sciences Nurse Anesthesia Program (NAP) invites applications for a part-time (PT) 0.5 FTE Professor of Practice Faculty member to join a well-established, successful group of nurse anesthesia educators. This non-tenure/non-status track position is designed for practicing clinicians with an interest in the faculty role of education and training of nurse anesthesia students. Individuals with experience and appropriate credentials are encouraged to apply. Successful candidates will be required to comply with internal academic policies and external accreditation agency standards and requirements to retain their appointment. This in-person Professor of Practice faculty position will require departmental teaching, administrative, and service responsibilities. This is a 12-month in-person PT position. Clinical faculty members have teaching and departmental service responsibilities and include: \u2022 assisting the Program Director and Assistant Program Director with the daily operations of the NAP. \u2022 ensuring compliance with and supporting activities related to accreditation by the Council on Accreditation of Nurse Anesthesia Educational Programs. \u2022 didactic and simulation instruction of students in the NAP. \u2022 advising students and assisting with doctoral scholarly projects. \u2022 assisting with program and curriculum development. \u2022 engaging in departmental committee work. \u2022 serving as faculty liaison for assigned clinical sites. \u2022 participating in continuing professional development. The teaching assignment for clinical faculty will depend on the Full-Time Equivalency (FTE) of the specific position. For a 1.0 FTE, the load should not exceed ten (10) courses per year for a 12-month appointment. Clinical faculty may also be hired at less than 1.0 FTE (based on 10 classes/year as 1.0 FTE) as determined by program needs. Overload guidelines apply as appropriate. \u2022 Doctoral degree in nursing/nurse anesthesia or related field, or evidence of progress toward a doctoral degree \u2022 Active certification as a Certified Registered Nurse Anesthetist (CRNA) \u2022 State licensure as a Registered Nurse \u2022 Minimum of 3-5 years of current full-time practice as a CRNA \u2022 Evidence of or progress toward formal instruction in curriculum, evaluation, and instruction \u2022 Strong computer skills, familiarity with Microsoft Office Suite (Word, PowerPoint, Excel, Teams), Adobe Acrobat, and Canvas preferred \u2022 Strong interpersonal skills and positive energy regarding collaboration \u2022 Established ability to work effectively as a team member \u2022 Excellent verbal, written, and electronic communication skills \u2022 Experience in clinical, didactic, and/or simulation instruction preferred \u2022 Enthusiastic and committed to the education and professional development of future CRNAs"}}, "rows": {"https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001232306506": {"school_id": 736, "posting_url": "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001232306506", "job_title_in_post": "Director of Student Accounts (Bursar)", "position_type": "Unclassified", "job_term": "Full-time", "department_or_school": "", "area_key_words": "Student Accounts", "deadline_of_application": "", "posted_date": "", "position_start_date": ""}, "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001232176700": {"school_id": 736, "posting_url": "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001232176700", "job_title_in_post": "Sr. IT Support Lead", "posit